res of the deceased to see if you can recall any specific person. His height is between 5 feet 9 inches and 10 inches, around sixty years old, with gray hair and brown eyes, clean-shaven beard, a thin face, and a strong chin. He is in good physical condition but not overweight. He was wearing a dark gray suit and well-groomed hands. He could be a banker, accountant, lawyer, or a professional in some industry. These descriptions remind you of anyone you know?\" [190] Miss Pemarcho carefully thought about the description before answering. [191] \"I cannot pinpoint it exactly because these are very common descriptions, and many people have these features. Perhaps I have seen or met someone like that in some occasion, but I cannot confirm who it is.\" [192] \"Have you received any letters recently from anyone planning to visit you?\" [193] \"Absolutely not.\" [194] \"Good. Then, you called the Cavendish Agency to find a typist -\"\n[195] She interrupted him. [196] \"I'm sorry. I didn't do that.\" [197] \"You didn't call the Cavendish Typing Agency and ask for \u2013\" Hakast's eyes widened. [198] \"I don't have a phone at home.\" [199] \"There's a phone booth at the end of the street,\" Detective Hakast pointed out. [200] \"Yes, there is. But I assure you, Detective Hakast, I don't need a typist, and I haven't \u2013 I repeat, I haven't called the Cavendish Typing Agency to make any such request.\" [201] \"Did you specifically ask for Miss Sheila Weber?\" [202] \"I've never heard of that name.\" [203] Detective Hakast stared at her, feeling quite surprised. [204] \"You didn't lock the front door when you left,\" he pointed out. [205] \"I do that often during the day.\" [206] \"Maybe someone could have walked in.\" [207] \"It seems someone did during this incident,\" Miss Pemukho said coldly. [208] \"Miss Pemukho, according to the evidence provided by the forensic expert, the man died between 1:30 and 2:45. Where were you during that time?\" [209] Miss Pemukho thought for a moment. [210] \"By 1:30, I was either out or getting ready to go out. I had to go buy some things.\" [211] \"Can you tell me exactly where you went?\" [212] \"Let me think. I went to the post office, a post office on Albion Road, to mail a package and buy some stamps; then I went to buy some everyday items, such as buttons and safety pins, at 'Field and Lane' fabric store; and then I came back. I can tell you the exact time I got home. When I got to the front gate, my cuckoo clock chimed three times 'cuckoo,' a sound that could be heard from the street.\" [213] \"What about your other clocks?\" [214] \"What did you say?\" [215] \"Your other clocks seem to be fast by more than an hour.\" [216] \"Fast? Do you mean the grandfather clock in the corner?\" [217] \"Not just that one, all the clocks in the living room are fast.\" [218] \"I don't know what you mean by 'other clocks'. There are no other clocks in this living room.\" [219] \"Chapter 3\"\n[220] Detective Hakast was flabbergasted. [221] \"Ah, so, Miss Pemshaw. What about the Dresden porcelain clock on the mantelpiece? And the exquisite French clock, gilded, and the silver travel clock, and, oh yes, the clock with 'Rosemary' written on the face.\" [222] This time, it was Miss Pemshaw's turn to be stunned. [223] \"Either you're going mad, or I am, Detective. I'm sure I don't have a Dresden porcelain clock, nor a French gilded clock, nor...what else did you say? Oh, right, the silver travel clock.\" [224] \"The silver travel clock,\" Detective Hakast mumbled. [225] \"I don't have that clock either. If you don't believe me, you can ask the woman who cleans my room. Her name is Mrs. Cotting.\" [226] Inspector Hakast was taken aback. Miss Pemarcho seemed very certain, her tone sharp and decisive, convincing. He took a moment to ponder the situation. Then he stood up. [227] \"I'd like to know, Miss Pemarcho, if you mind coming with me to the next room to see these clocks?\" [228] \"Of course not. Frankly, I'd like 