in the same way. [247] \"There is usually only the grandfather clock in the room, placed in the corner next to the window\u2014\"\n[248] \"Yes.\" [249] \"And there is a cuckoo clock on the wall near the door.\" [250] Hakaste found himself at a loss for words after that. He observed the woman in front of him, knowing she would not respond to his scrutiny, and felt a sense of relief. Her forehead was slightly furrowed in confusion. She suddenly spoke:\n[251] \"I don't understand what's going on. I really can't make sense of it.\" [252] She extended her hand, knowing exactly where she was in the room, and quickly found a chair to sit down. Hakaste noticed the fingerprint technician standing by the door. [253] \"Did you check all the clocks?\" he asked. [254] \"I checked everything, sir. The gilded clock has no fingerprints, which is normal since the surface of that material doesn't leave prints. The same goes for the porcelain clock. But there are no fingerprints on the leather travel clock or the silver clock, which is quite unusual. Moreover, all the clocks are not wound up, and they all stopped at the same time, 4:13 PM.\" [255] \"What about the other rooms?\" [256] \"There were three to four different types of fingerprints found in the room, and it's likely that they all belong to women. All the items found in the deceased's pockets were placed on the table.\" [257] Pointing in the direction of the person's head, he saw a small pile of items placed on the table. Hakaste walked over to take a closer look. There was a small wallet containing seven pounds, ten shillings, and some change, an unmarked silk handkerchief, a small box of digestive pills, and a business card. Hakaste bent down to look at it. [258] R. H. Kouri, Esq. [259] Great City Small Town Insurance Company\n[260] 7 Danver Street,\n[261] London W2. [262] Hakakst returned to the sofa where Miss Pemuko was sitting. [263] \"Do you ever make appointments to see someone from the insurance company?\" [264] \"Insurance company? No, absolutely not.\" [265] \"What about 'Metropolitan District Insurance Company'?\" Hakakst said. [266] Miss Pemaroo shook her head. \"I've never heard of that company.\" [267] \"Have you ever considered getting any kind of insurance?\" [268] \"No. I have fire and theft insurance with 'Jupiter Insurance Company', which has a branch here, and I don't have life insurance. I have no family or close relatives, so there's no need to insure my life.\" [269] \"I see,\" Hakaste said. \"So, does the name Kirby have any connection to you? Mr. R. H. Kirby?\" [270] \"Kirby?\" she repeated the name and shook her head. \"That's not a common name, is it? No, I don't think I've ever heard that name or known anyone by that name. Is that the name of the deceased?\" [271] \"Maybe,\" said Hakaste. [272] Miss Pemmerhoven hesitated for a moment. Then she said:\n[273] \"Should I go, touch\u2014\"\n[274] He quickly understood her meaning. [275] \"You mean to 'feel' it yourself, Miss Pemmerhoven? Is that too much to ask of you? I'm not very good at doing such things, but compared to a verbal description, your fingers might give you a better idea of what the person looked like.\" [276] \"Yes, that's what I mean,\" said Miss Pemuk. \"I admit that doing this won't be pleasant, but if you think it will be helpful, I'm willing to do it.\" [277] \"Thank you,\" said Hakast. \"I'll take you there.\" [278] He led her around the sofa, indicating for her to kneel down and then gently guiding her hand to the dead man's face. She was very calm and emotionless. Her fingers ran over the dead man's hair and eyes, lingering for a moment at the left ear before moving on to the bridge of the nose, mouth, and chin. Then she shook her head and stood up. [279] \"I have a clear image of his appearance now,\" she said. \"But I'm sure I've never seen him before, I don't recognize him.\" [280] The fingerprint examiner packed up his toolbox, left the room, and poked his head back in. [281] \"They're going to move him,\" he said, indicating the corpse. \"Can he be moved?\" [282] \"Move him,\" Detective Hakaste said. \"Come and sit over here, Miss Pemukho.\" [283] He arranged her to sit in a chair in the corner. Two men entered the room. Soon, Mr. Kuri's body was carried out. Detective Hakaste went outside to the front door and then returned to the living room. He sat next to Miss Pemukho. [284] \"This is quite a strange case, Miss Pemukho,\" he said. \"I'd like to go over the key points of the incident again to make sure I have it right. If I've said anything wrong, please correct me. You didn't have any appointments for any visitors today, you didn't consult or intend to take out any kind of insurance, and you didn't receive any letters saying that an insurance company's representative would be visiting you today, right?\" [285] \"Absolutely correct.\" [286] \"You did not need a stenographer's service, and you did not call the Calverdish Document Service and request someone to come here at three o'clock.\" [287] \"That is correct.\" [288] \"When you left the house at approximately one thirty, there were only two clocks in the house, the cuckoo clock and the grandfather clock. There were no other clocks.\" [289] Just as Miss Pemaroux was about to answer \"yes,\" she stopped. [290] \"If I am required to give a completely accurate answer, I cannot guarantee it. Since I cannot see, I cannot be certain of anything that was not in the house when I last cleaned it, or anything that has disappeared. That means the last time I can confirm that everything was in its place was when I cleaned the house this morning, and there was nothing out of the ordinary. I usually clean this room myself because the cleaning ladies always forget to clean the ornaments.\" [291] \"Did you go out this morning?\" [292] \"Yes. I went to Allenberg College, as usual, at about ten o'clock. I had classes there until twelve fifteen. I got back home around twelve forty-five, and I made myself some scrambled eggs and a cup of tea, and then I went out again, like I said, around one thirty. I ate in the kitchen, and I might add that I didn't go back into this room.\" [293] \"I see,\" said Harkness. \"Since you're sure there were no extra clocks here before ten o'clock this morning, they must have been brought in later.\" [294] \"You can ask my cleaning lady, Mrs. Kutting. She usually comes here at about ten o'clock and leaves around twelve. She lives at 17 Debo Street.\" [295] \"Thank you, Miss Pemuko. Now, let's move on to the next question. At some point today, four clocks were brought here, and their hands were set to four thirteen. Does that time mean anything to you?\" [296] \"Four thirteen,\" Miss Pemerton shook her head. \"Nothing at all.\" [297] \"Now let's talk about the victim. If your maid let him in and left him here in the room, it seems impossible unless you had told her he was coming to see you, which we will confirm with her. If he came to see you for business or personal reasons between 1:30 and 2:45, and you say you don't know about it; if he came to discuss insurance, but you say that's impossible. The door was unlocked, so he could come in and sit and wait for you to return. Why did he do that?\" [298] \"This whole thing is ridiculous,\" Miss Pemerton said impatiently. \"So you think Mr. Corry brought those clocks with him?\" [299] \"There were no bags found,\" said Hercule Poirot. \"He couldn't have had four clocks in his pockets. Miss Pemerton, think carefully now, think of anything related to clocks or any suggestions about time. Four thirteen. Fourteen minutes past four. Is that what you meant?\" [300] She shook her head. [301] \"I've been trying to tell myself it was a madman or someone who got the wrong house, but even then, it doesn't explain everything. Inspector, I can't help you.\" [302] A young policeman poked his head in. Hakaste went out. The young policeman waited in the hallway, and they walked together to the front door, talking for a few minutes. [303] \"You can take this young lady home now,\" Hakaste said. \"The address is 14 Palmerston Road.\" [304] He came back, walked into the dining room. The door to the kitchen was open, and he could hear Miss Pemaroo washing something in the sink. He stood at the door. [305] \"I want to take those clocks with me, Miss Pemaroo. I'll give you a receipt.\" [306] \"Of course, Detective, they weren't mine to begin with.\" [307] Hakast turns to Miss Webber. [308] \"You can go home now, Miss Webber. The police car will take you.\" [309] Miss Webber and Mr. Collins stand up. [310] \"Take her to the car, Mr. Collins?\" Hakast says, pulling a chair up to the table and quickly writing a receipt. [311] Colin and Shira left and walked down the path. Shira suddenly stopped. [312] \"My gloves. I forgot them\u2014\"\n[313] \"I'll get them for you.\" [314] \"No, I know where I put them. Now I'm not worried, they've moved 'it' away.\" [315] She ran back and came out shortly.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1] Chapter 1\n[2] Colin Lamb's Narrative\n[3] 1\n[4] According to police records: At 2:59 PM on September 9th, I was walking west on Wilbraham Crescent. This was my first time here, and I must admit, everything about the place left me bewildered. [5] For some time now, I've had a feeling, and this feeling has been growing stronger and stronger, almost impossible to shake off. That's just how I felt. [6] I was looking for a house numbered 61, and I thought, maybe I won't find it. I had carefully found houses from 1 to 35, and just then, I discovered that Wilbraham Crescent had reached its end. A road called Albany Avenue rudely blocked my path. I turned around. There were no houses to the north, only a wall. Behind this wall, there were towering modern apartments, and it was clear that the entrance was on another road. It was impossible to find a way from here. [7] As I walked, I paid attention to the house numbers I passed by \u2013 24, 23, 22, 21, Diana's Cottage (which must be number 20, with an orange cat washing itself on the gatepost), and 19. [8] The door to number 19 suddenly opened, and a young girl rushed out, then ran up the path. I thought a missile's speed was nothing compared to hers. She ran with such a sharp cry that she looked even more like a missile. The sound was high-pitched and pitiful. The girl ran through the gate and collided with me, almost knocking me off the sidewalk. She not only collided with me but also grabbed me tightly, desperately clinging to me. [9] \"Take it easy,\" I said, gently shaking her when I regained my balance, \"Now take it easy.\" [10] The girl's emotions finally calmed down. Although she was still holding on to me tightly, she was no longer screaming, but only panting heavily, gasping and sobbing. [11] In this situation, I must say that my reaction was not quick enough. I asked her what happened. Realizing that my tone was too weak, I immediately changed it. [12] \"What happened?\" [13] The girl took a deep breath. [14] \"Over there!\" She pointed to the place behind her. [15] \"Then what?\" [16] \"There was a man lying on the floor... dead... she was about to step on him.\" [17] \"Who? How did this happen?\" [18] \"I think, because she's blind. And there was blood on him.\" [19] \"Indeed, there was blood.\" I said, looking at the stain on my sleeve. \"Now I'm the same.\" I pointed to the stain. Thinking about the current situation, I sighed. \"You should take me there to see.\" [20] But she began to shake her head vigorously. [21] \"I can't go, I can't go... I don't want to go back there.\" [22] \"Maybe you're right.\" I looked around. There didn't seem to be a place to put a girl who was about to faint. I gently guided her, letting her lean against the iron railing. [23] \"Stay here,\" I said, \"don't leave until I come back. I won't be long.\" I leaned forward and if you feel uncomfortable, put your head between your knees, it will feel better.\" [24] \"I, I think I'm okay now.\" [25] Although she said so, she was still a little shaken, but I didn't want to talk to her too much. I gently patted her shoulder, comforted her, and then walked quickly up the path. I went through the door into the house, hesitated slightly in the hallway, observed the left door, which was an empty restaurant, and then walked through the foyer into the opposite living room. [26] The first thing I saw was an old woman with gray hair sitting on a chair. When I went in, her head turned quickly towards me and said,\n[27] \"Who came?\" [28] I immediately realized that the woman's eyes were blind. Her eyes looked straight at me, then swept past me and landed on the back of my left ear. [29] I said directly:\n[30] \"A young girl ran up the street, saying that someone died here.\" [31] I felt that what I said was absurd. In this clean room, the woman sat with her hands folded on the chair, looking so calm. It seemed impossible to find a dead man here. [32] But she immediately answered. [33] \"Behind the sofa,\" she said. [34] I went around the corner of the sofa. Then I saw the open arms, the vacant eyes, and the congealed blood clots. [35] \"How did this happen?\" I suddenly asked. [36] \"I don't know.\" [37] \"So, who is he?\" [38] \"I don't know.\" [39] \"We have to call the police.\" I look around. \"Where's the phone?\" \"I don't have a phone.\" [40] I stare at her intently. [41] \"Are you living here? Is this your house?\" [42] \"Yes.\" [43] \"Can you tell me what happened?\" [44] \"Of course. I came back with my shopping -\" I noticed the shopping bag thrown on the chair near the door. \"I came home and immediately sensed that there was someone else in the house. A blind person is very sensitive to such things. I asked who was in the house. No one answered me. I only heard someone breathing heavily. I went towards the source of the sound. Then someone yelled out - someone had died, I was about to step on the body. Then someone screamed past me, running out of the room.\" [45] I nodded. They both told the same story. [46] \"What did you do then?\" [47] \"I walked carefully until my foot touched something.\" [48] \"What happened next?\" [49] \"I squatted down. I felt a man's hand. It was cold and there was no pulse\u2026 I got up and walked over here, sat down and waited for someone to come. It won't be long, someone will come. That young woman, whoever she is, will call for help. I think it's best not to leave this house.\" [50] I was shocked by the woman's calmness. She didn't scream or panic in the house, she just sat there quietly waiting. She was rational, but we still had to take action. [51] She questioned:\n[52] \"Who are you?\" [53] \"My name is Colin Lamb. I just happened to pass by.\" [54] \"Where did the young girl go?\" [55] \"I let her rest near the entrance. She was scared. Where is the nearest place to call for help?\" [56] \"Follow this path for about fifty yards, and there's a phone booth just before the corner.\" [57] \"Yes, I remember passing by there. I'm going to call the police. You\u2014\" I hesitated. [58] I didn't know whether to say \"Are you going to stay here?\" or \"Are you okay?\" [59] She spared me the decision. [60] \"You should bring the girl inside,\" she said firmly. [61] \"I don't know if she'll come,\" I said skeptically. [62] \"Of course, don't let her come into this room. Tell her to wait in the dining room next to the entrance. Tell her I'm brewing tea for her.\" [63] She approached me. [64] \"But can you do that?\" she asked. [65] A slight smirk appeared on her face. [66] \"My dear young man, since I moved into this house fourteen years ago, I have been cooking in the kitchen myself. Being blind doesn't mean I'm useless.\" [67] \"I'm sorry, I was too ignorant. Perhaps I can ask for your name?\" [68] \"Millee Pema-Chhoe \u2013 Miss.\" [69] I left the room and walked out along the path. The girl saw me and struggled to get up. [70] \"I, I think I'm almost better now.\" [71] I helped her stand up, smiling,\n[72] \"That's good.\" [73] \"There, there, someone died, right?\" [74] I immediately nodded. [75] \"Indeed. I'm going to the phone booth to report the incident to the police. If I were you, I would wait in the house. Go to the restaurant and wait on your left as you enter. Miss Millechamp is brewing tea for you.\" I raised my voice to prevent her from immediately contradicting me. \"Wait in the restaurant, on your left as you enter. Miss Millechamp is brewing tea for you.\" [76] \"So that's Miss Pemakh, is it? Can she see?\" [77] \"Yes, she is. This has certainly shocked her, but she has been very rational about it. Come on, I'll take you in. Having a cup of tea while waiting for the police will make you feel better.\" [78] I put my arm around her shoulder and led her down the path. After settling her comfortably at the table, I rushed to the phone booth. [79] 2\n[80] A cold voice said, \"Croedin Police Station.\" [81] \"Is Inspector Hatchet there?\" [82] The voice suddenly became cautious:\n[83] \"I don't know. Who is this?\" [84] \"Tell him it's Colin Loom.\" [85] \"Please wait.\" [86] I waited. Then Dick Hakast's voice came over the line. [87] \"Is that Colin? It's been a while. Where are you?\" [88] \"Crofton. To be precise, I'm on the floor of 19 Wilbraham Crescent. There's a man lying there, dead, I think stabbed. Probably died about half an hour ago.\" [89] \"Who found him? You?\" [90] \"No, a stranger. A girl came running out of the house, almost knocking me over. She said there was a man lying on the floor of the house, dead. A blind woman almost stepped on his body.\" [91] \"You're not pulling my leg, are you?\" Dick asked skeptically. [92] \"I admit, it sounds incredible, but that's the truth. The woman is the owner of the house, Miss Mirella Pembleton.\" [93] \"The one who almost tripped over the dead man?\" [94] \"Not like you think. If she was blind, she wouldn't have noticed the dead man lying there.\" [95] \"I'll be right there. Wait for me there. How did you take care of the girl?\" [96] \"Miss Pemah is brewing tea for her.\" [97] Dic commented that the arrangement sounded somewhat soothing. [98] Chapter Two\n[99] At 19 Wilbraham Crescent, the police officers had arrived. A forensic expert, a photographer, and a fingerprint examiner were present. They worked in an orderly manner, with each person performing their designated task efficiently. [100] The last to arrive was Inspector Hakast, a tall man with expressive eyebrows and a stern expression. He observed that all the work he had arranged was proceeding smoothly. He glanced at the corpse, exchanged a few brief words with the forensic expert, and then went to the dining room. There, he found three people seated with three empty teacups in front of them. They were Miss Pemah, Colin Lum, and a tall girl with brown curls. The girl's eyes were wide with fear. \"Very nice,\" the inspector remarked in his usual witty manner. [101] He introduced himself to Miss Pema. [102] \"Detective Inspector Dick Hakast.\" [103] He knew something about Miss Pema, despite their lines of work never intersecting. He had seen her a few times before, knowing her to be a teacher at the Allenburg Institute, teaching blind script to disabled children. The discovery of a man dead of murder in such a clean, simple house seemed improbable. But often the most improbable things are the most common. [104] \"This is a truly awful thing, Miss Pema,\" he said, \"and I'm afraid it's quite a shock to you. I need to get a clear understanding of the whole incident from you. I know -\" he glanced quickly at the police report he had been given, \"Hilary Webb was the one who actually found the body. Miss Pema, if you allow me to use your kitchen, I would like to bring Miss Webb there and speak with her privately.\" [105] He opened the door connecting the dining room and the kitchen, waiting for the girl to enter. A young plainclothes detective was already in the kitchen, sitting at a table with a Formica-topped surface, writing something quietly. [106] \"This chair looks quite comfortable,\" Hakcastle said, pulling a modern-style Windsor chair. [107] Hilary Webber sat nervously, her big fearful eyes staring straight at him. [108] Hakcastle almost said, \"Dear, I won't eat you,\" but he held back and said instead,\n[109] \"Don't worry. We just want to know what happened. Your name is Hilary Webber. Your address?\" [110] \"14 Palmerston Road, a little further down the gas works.\" [111] \"Okay, next. I assume you have a job?\" [112] \"Yes, I'm a stenographer typist, and I work at Miss Martin's Document Printing Agency.\" [113] \"Cavendish Document Printing Agency, is that the full name?\" [114] \"Yes.\" [115] \"How long have you been working there?\" [116] \"About a year. Well, to be exact, ten months.\" [117] \"Okay. Now tell me, how did you end up at 19 Wilbraham Crescent today?\" [118] \"Well, it's like this. Miss Pember called the typewriting agency and asked for a stenographer to come here at 3 pm. So when I came back from lunch, Miss Martin sent me here.\" [119] \"Is that just a regular shift, or do you guys arrange these kinds of jobs differently?\" [120] \"Actually, it's not exactly like that. Miss Pember specifically asked for me to come.\" [121] \"Ms. Pemakh specifically asked for you to come.\" Mr. Hakast's eyebrows remained unmoving. \"I see, because you worked for her before?\" [122] \"But I didn't.\" Sheila immediately answered. [123] \"You didn't work for her before? Are you sure?\" [124] \"Yes, I'm positive. I mean, she's not the kind of person you forget. That's what's strange about it.\" [125] \"It is quite unusual. Well, let's not discuss that for now. When did you get here?\" [126] \"It must have been around three o'clock, because that cuckoo c