sn't it?\" [1876] Chapter 18\n[1877] 1\n[1878] When Hercule Poirot arrived at 19 Wilbraham Crescent, Miss Pembridge was just about to leave. [1879] \"I'm sorry to keep you, Miss Pembridge.\" [1880] \"Oh. You're \u2013 Inspector Hercule Poirot?\" [1881] \"Yes. Can I talk to you?\" [1882] \"I don't want to be late for school. Will it take long?\" [1883] She went inside, and he followed her. [1884] \"Did you hear about what happened this afternoon?\" he said. [1885] \"What happened?\" [1886] \"I thought you would have heard. A girl was killed in the phone booth down this way.\" [1887] \"Murdered? When?\" [1888] \"Two hours and forty-five minutes ago.\" He looked at the grandfather clock. [1889] \"I haven't heard anything about it, not at all.\" Miss Pemukh said, her voice suddenly sounding angry. It seemed that the girl's misfortune had touched her heart. \"A girl, murdered! What kind of girl?\" [1890] \"Her name is Ina Brent, she works at Cavendish Document Printing.\" [1891] \"Another girl from there! Was she also arranged to come here, her name is Sheila something?\" [1892] \"I don't think so,\" the detective said. \"Did she come here to see you?\" [1893] \"Come here? No, of course not.\" [1894] \"If she had come here, were you there?\" [1895] \"I'm not sure. What time are you talking about?\" [1896] \"Around 12:30 or a little later.\" [1897] \"Yes,\" said Miss Pemukho, \"I was at home then.\" [1898] \"Where did you go after the trial?\" [1899] \"I came straight back here.\" She paused and then asked, \"Why do you think this girl might have come to see me?\" [1900] \"Well, she was at the hearing this morning. She saw you. There must be a reason she came to Wilbraham Crescent. As far as we know, she doesn't know anyone on this street.\" [1901] \"But why did she come to find me just because she saw me in the interrogation room?\" [1902] \"Hmm...,\" the detective smiled slightly, quickly covering up the smile as he realized Miss Pema didn't like his attempts to get close to her. \"No one will ever know the reason now. Perhaps she just wanted a signature, or something similar.\" [1903] \"A signature!\" Miss Pemukh's voice was filled with contempt, and then she said, \"Yes... yes, I suppose you're right. Such things do happen.\u201d Then she shook her head briskly. \"But I can assure you, Inspector Hakaste, that such a thing has not happened today, and since I've been back from the interrogation, no one has come here.\u201d\n[1904] \"Well, thank you, Miss Pema. We thought it best to check every possibility.\" [1905] \"How old is she?\" Miss Pemaux asked. [1906] \"I believe she's nineteen years old.\" [1907] \"Nineteen? So young. Very young... poor child. Who could want to harm such a young girl?\" [1908] \"But it did happen,\" Detective Hardcastle said. [1909] \"Was she pretty... charming... seductive?\" [1910] \"No,\" Detective Hardcastle said. \"I think she wished to be, but she wasn't.\" [1911] \"Then it wasn't for that reason,\" Miss Pembroke said, shaking her head again. \"I'm sorry, I'm sorry, Detective Hakast, I couldn't help you.\" [1912] He left, as before, once again impressed by Miss Pembroke's personality. [1913] 2\n[1914] Miss Wutherspoon was also at home. She was still the same, suddenly opening the door as if deliberately tempting someone to do something they shouldn't. [1915] \"Oh, it's you!\" she said. \"In fact, I've told your people everything I know.\" [1916] \"I'm sure you've answered all the questions asked of you,\" Detective Hakast said. \"But not all questions can be answered at once, you know. We wanted to know some details.\" [1917] \"I don't understand why,\" Miss Walters said, looking at him with a nearly critical eye, as if it were all his fault. \"Please come in, please come in. You can't stand on the doormat all day. Come in, sit down, and ask whatever you want to ask. Although, to be honest, some questions I don't know. As I said, I went out to make a phone call, and as soon as I opened the door of the phone booth, I found the girl. I've never been so scared in my life. I rushed out to find the police. After that, if you want to know, I came back here and drank an equivalent of a medicinal dose of brandy, medicinally.\" [1918] \"You're very wise, madam,\" Detective Hakast said. [1919] \"That's all there is to it,\" Miss Walters said. [1920] \"I'd like to ask if you're absolutely sure you've never seen the girl before?\" [1921] \"I may have seen her many times,\" said Miss Watkins, \"but I don't remember. I mean, she may have served me at Woolworth's, or sat next to me on the bus, or sold me a ticket at the cinema.\" [1922] \"She was a stenographer at Cavenish Typewriting Agency.\" [1923] \"I don't think I've ever had the opportunity to use a stenographer. Maybe she worked at my brother's 'Gatesford and Swinburne Agency.' Is that what you want to know?\" [1924] \"No, no,\" said Detective Hercaster, \"that has nothing to do with it. I just want to know if she came to see you before she was killed this morning.\" [1925] \"To see me? No, of course not. Why would she do that?\" [1926] \"Well, we don't know about that,\" Detective Hacker said, \"but someone said they saw her come to your front gate this morning. Did they get it wrong?\" [1927] \"Someone saw her come to my front gate? Nonsense.\" [1928] \"What?\" [1929] \"Well, I guess she might have slipped a flyer or something through the door crack. I saw a flyer at lunchtime, about a disarmament conference. I think. Things happen every day. I guess she probably came by, put something in the mailbox, but you can't blame me for that, can you?\" [1930] \"Of course not. Now, let's talk about your phone. You said it was broken. The phone exchange says there's no such thing.\" [1931] \"The telephone exchange can say anything! When I dialed, there was a strange buzzing noise, not a busy signal, so I went to the phone booth.\" [1932] Poirot stood up. [1933] \"I'm sorry to disturb you like this, Miss Whitehouse, but this girl coming to New Moon Street might really have been visiting someone and gone to a house not far from here.\" [1934] \"So you should ask along New Moon Street,\" said Miss Whitehouse. \"I think she's most likely to have gone to the one next door \u2013 I mean Miss Pemarue.\" [1935] \"Why do you think that's the most likely?\" [1936] \"You said she was a stenographer from Cavendish Typewriting Agency. Of course, if I'm not mistaken, I heard just a few days ago, on the day that man was killed, Miss Pembury had a stenographer come to her.\" [1937] \"She denied it.\" [1938] \"Well, if you ask me,\" Miss Wattershouse said, \"I would say she might be a little odd - I mean Miss Pembury. I think, perhaps, she might have called the typing agency and asked for a stenographer to come to her. Then, perhaps, she forgot all about it.\" [1939] \"Do you think she could be the murderer?\" [1940] \"I never thought she'd go around killing someone or anything like that. I know someone was killed in her house, but I never suggested that had anything to do with Miss Pembury. No. I was just thinking she might have some odd preferences, like some other people. I know a woman who keeps calling the candy store and ordering a dozen sugar cookies. But she really doesn't need them, and when they're delivered, she says she didn't order them. Just that kind of thing.\" [1941] \"Of course, anything's possible.\" Hercast said. He left Miss Warton after bidding her goodbye. [1942] He thought she was just trying to clear herself of suspicion. In other words, if she believed someone saw the girl go into her house, then suggesting that the girl went into No. 19 becomes a clever way of covering up in this particular case. [1943] Hercast glanced at his watch and decided he had time to visit the Cavenish Document Printing Office. He knew they would be back at work at two o'clock after lunch. He might be able to get some help from the other girls there, and he could see Miss Sheila Webber. [1944] 3\n[1945] When he entered the office, one of the girls immediately stood up. [1946] \"Are you Inspector Hackett?\" she asked. \"Miss Martin is waiting for you.\" [1947] She led him into the inner office, where Miss Martin immediately began to berate him. [1948] \"This is simply disgraceful, Inspector Hackett, absolutely disgraceful! You must get to the bottom of this, you must get to the bottom of it immediately! Stop dilly-dallying! It's the police's duty to protect the public, and that's exactly what we need now. Protection. I want my girls to be protected, and we urgently need protection!\" [1949] \"Certainly, Miss Martin, that\u2014\"\n[1950] \"Are you going to deny that my two girls are victims? It's obvious, someone irresponsible, with some kind of, well, what they call these days, a perverted psychology, targets shorthand typists or typewriting agencies. They deliberately torture them. First, Miss Webber was cruelly teased, sent to discover a corpse, something that might drive a nervous girl insane. Now this has happened. A girl so innocent and kind was murdered in a telephone booth. You must investigate thoroughly, Inspector.\" [1951] \"What I want to do most now is to get to the bottom of this, Miss Martindeer. I came to see if you could give me some help.\" [1952] \"Help! What help can I give you? If I could help, I would have found you by now! You must find out who killed this poor girl, Iena, and who played this cruel trick on Miss Hila. I have high standards for my girls, Inspector, I expect them to be diligent and not be late or disheveled. But I cannot tolerate them being killed or involved in murder. I want to protect them, and I want to see the people paid by the state protect them so they can work in peace. She glared at him, looking like a furious tigress. [1953] \"Give us some time, Miss Martindeer.\" he said. [1954] \"Time? I guess you think you have all the time in the world because a silly girl died. The next one to be murdered will be another of these girls.\" [1955] \"I think you don't need to worry about that, Miss Martindeer.\" [1956] \"I suppose you didn't think this girl would be killed this morning, did you, Detective? If you had, you would have taken preventive measures to protect her. You would have been just as surprised when one of my girls was killed or found in a terrible compromising situation. The whole thing is so unusual, incredible! You must admit it's a mad scheme. That means, maybe what you read in the papers is true. Like those clocks. I noticed that nobody mentioned them at the trial this morning.\" [1957] \"We had to keep the questioning to a minimum this morning, Miss Martindale. It was just a postponement of the trial, you know.\" [1958] \"All I want to say is, Miss Martindale, that you have to take action on this.\" [1959] \"Didn't Ina give you any hints? Didn't she show any concern about something? Didn't she talk to you about it?\" [1960] \"I don't think she would have talked to me even if she was worried,\" said Miss Martindale. \"But what was she worried about?\" [1961] This was exactly what Inspector Hercule Poirot wanted to know. He knew he wouldn't get an answer from Miss Martin. So he said:\n[1962] \"I want to talk to each girl here. I understand Ina Brent wouldn't confide her fears or worries to you, but she might have confided in her colleagues.\" [1963] \"That's probably true,\" Miss Martin said. \"They're always gossiping - these girls. As soon as they hear my footsteps in the corridor, all the typewriters start working. But what were they doing before that? Chattering, chattering, never stopping! \"After a moment's pause, she said, \"There are only three girls in the office now. Do you want to talk to them now? The others are out on business. I can give you their names and addresses if you need them.\" [1964] \"Thank you, Miss Martin.\" [1965] \"I hope you talk to them alone,\" Miss Martin said. \"If I stand here and watch, they'll feel uncomfortable. Because, you understand, then they'll have to admit they've been gossiping and wasting time.\" [1966] She rose from her chair and opened the door, entering the outer office. [1967] \"Girls,\" she said, \"Inspector Hakast wants to talk to you. You stop what you're doing. Talk to the inspector properly and see if you can help him find the killer of Ina Brent.\u201d\n[1968] She returned to her office and slammed the door. Three girls, looking frightened, stared at the inspector. One was blonde, wearing glasses, and looked trustworthy but not very intelligent. One looked mischievous, with dark skin and hair, her hair disheveled as if she had just been through a storm. Her eyes darted around the room, and he suspected that what she said might not be entirely truthful. Everything had to be handled carefully. [1969] He spoke calmly and casually. [1970] \"Have you all heard about what happened to your colleague Ina Brent?\u201d\n[1971] Three heads nodded vigorously. [1972] \"By the way, how did you find out?\" [1973] They looked at each other, deciding who should speak first. After unanimous agreement, the blonde girl spoke. Her name was apparently Janet. [1974] \"Ina didn't come back to work at two o'clock as usual,\" she explained. [1975] \"The cat was furious,\" said the black-haired girl, Melvin, before correcting herself, \"I mean, Miss Martin.\" [1976] The third girl chuckled. \"The cat is our nickname for her,\" she explained. [1977] \"Not a bad name,\" the detective thought. [1978] \"She can be quite terrifying when she's angry,\" Melanie said, \"She'll scold you fiercely. She asked us if Ina had said she wouldn't come to the office today and said she should at least give a reason.\" [1979] The blonde girl said, \"I told Miss Martinfield that she went with us to the courthouse, but we didn't see her after that, and we don't know where she went.\" [1980] \"That's true, isn't it?\" Hakaste asked, \"You don't know where she went after leaving the courthouse.\" [1981] \"I suggested we all go to lunch,\" Morin said. \"But she seemed preoccupied. She said she wasn't sure if she should eat lunch or buy something to eat in the office.\" [1982] \"So, she meant she'd come back to the office after that?\" [1983] \"Oh, yes, of course. We all have to come back to work.\" [1984] \"Has anyone noticed anything different about Ina Brent these past few days? Does she seem troubled, like she's thinking about something? Has she told you what's bothering her? If you know anything, please tell me.\" [1985] They looked at each other, their exchange lacking any attempt at concealment. It seemed to be merely vague speculation. [1986] \"She's often been in a state of mind, \" said Molly. \"She's been making mistakes and getting things mixed up. Her reactions are always a beat behind.\" [1987] \"It seems like there's always some kind of trouble with Iona,\" said the smiling girl. \"Remember when her high heel fell off a few days ago? Things like that always happen to Iona.\" [1988] \"I remember,\" said Hakast.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Can we sit down?\" [969] \"Oh, yes, please, sit, sit.\" [970] Mrs. Lymsey sat down on a straight-backed chair, impatiently watching them. She thought this would not take only a little while. [971] \"You don't have to stay here.\" Inspector Hakast said kindly to the two children. [972] \"Oh, we don't want to go.\" Bill said. [973] \"We don't want to go.\" Ted echoed. [974] \"We want to hear you.\" Bill said. [975] \"Yes.\" Ted said. [976] \"Is there a lot of blood there?\" Bill asked. [977] \"Was it a thief?\" Ted said. [978] \"Be quiet, children,\" Mrs. Lyme said. \"Didn't you hear? Mr. Hakaste said he doesn't need you here.\" [979] \"We don't want to leave,\" Bill said. \"We want to hear.\" [980] Hakaste walked towards the door, opened it, and looked at the two children. [981] \"Go out,\" he said. [982] Just two words, spoken calmly and authoritatively, yet a symbol of authority. The two children rose and shuffled out of the room. [983] \"How remarkable,\" Mrs. Rymes thought, with a heartfelt admiration. \"But why can't I do that?\" [984] She pondered on it, for she was their mother. She had heard that these two children behaved completely differently outside than they did at home. Only a mother could make things so difficult. But perhaps, she reflected, it was better this way. If they were friendly, quiet, considerate, and polite at home, and turned into little thugs outside, it would be worse, yes, much worse. When Mr. Hakcastle Detective returned and sat down again, she remembered the purpose of their visit. [985] \"If it's about what happened yesterday at number 19,\" she said nervously, \"I really have nothing to say, Detective. I know nothing about it. I don't even know the people who live there.\" [986] \"The house is inhabited by Miss Pemaroux. She is blind and works at Allenberg College.\" [987] \"Oh, is that so?\" Mrs. Lymes said. \"I'm afraid I don't know anyone who lives on the lower half of New Moon Street.\" [988] \"Were you here between twelve thirty and three o'clock yesterday?\" [989] \"Oh, I was,\" Mrs. Lymes said. \"I had to cook and do other things. But I left before three. I took the children to the cinema.\" [990] The inspector took a photograph from his pocket and handed it to him. [991] \"Can you tell me if you've ever seen this person before?\" [992] Mrs. Lymes looked at it with some interest. [993] \"No,\" she said, \"no, I don't think I have. I can't remember seeing him.\" [994] \"Did he come to visit you for some reason? Try to sell you insurance, or something like that?\" [995] Mrs. Lymes shook her head firmly. [996] \"No. No, I'm quite sure he hasn't been here.\" [997] \"His name, we seem to have a little clue, is Mr. R. H. Corey.\" [998] He looked at her questioningly. Mrs. Rymes shook her head