e continued, \"keeping the children happy and well-fed during the holidays. When do they go back to school?\" [1015] \"They go back tomorrow,\" Mrs. Rymes said. [1016] \"I hope you can have a good rest now.\" [1017] \"That means I have to be lazy,\" she said. [1018] The other young man, who had been quietly taking notes, stared at her for a moment and then spoke, startling her. [1019] \"You should find a foreign girl,\" he said, \"an au pair, as they call them, to do some chores here and learn English.\" [1020] \"I hope I could try,\" said Mrs. Rymes, considering, \"I always thought foreigners were difficult to deal with. My husband often teases me about it. But in this case, he knows more about it than I do. I don't travel abroad as much as he does.\" [1021] \"He's out now, isn't he?\" Hakast said. [1022] \"Yes. He went to Sweden in early August. He's an engineer. It's a pity he left when the holidays just started. He's so good with the children. He even likes playing with electric trains more than the children. Along the railway line, the switching stations, it smoothly passes through the hallway and enters another room. It's hard not to trip over it.\" She shook her head, \"Men are also children.\" She said with a doting tone. [1023] \"Do you know when he'll be back, Mrs. Rimmers?\" [1024] \"I never know,\" she sighed, \"It's hard to know.\" Her voice trembled slightly. Colin looked at her sharply. [1025] \"We can't take up any more of your time, Mrs. Rimmers.\" [1026] Hakaste stood up. [1027] \"Perhaps your children could show us around the garden?\" [1028] Bill and Ted were waiting in the hallway and immediately responded to the suggestion. [1029] \"Of course, they can,\" Bill apologized, \"but the garden isn't very big.\" [1030] It was evident that the garden of 62 Wilbraham Crescent had been well laid out before, with an orderly appearance. On one side were beds of dahlias and Michaelmas daisies, followed by a somewhat unevenly maintained lawn. The paths needed to be weeded, and models of planes, rocket launchers, and other representations of modern science were scattered around, looking worn out. At the end of the garden was an apple tree, with red apples hanging from it. Next to it was a pear tree. [1031] \"That's the house,\" Ted said, pointing to the space between the apple tree and the pear tree, from which you could clearly see the rear of Miss Pemshaw's house. \"That's where the murder took place at number 19.\" [1032] \"Can you see that house clearly?\" the detective asked. \"I imagine from the upstairs windows it would be even clearer.\" [1033] \"You're right,\" Bill said. \"If we had been up there yesterday and looked out the window, we might have seen something. But we didn't.\" [1034] \"We were watching a movie,\" Ted said. [1035] \"Did you leave any fingerprints?\" Bill asked. [1036] \"No useful fingerprints were found. Did you go to the garden yesterday?\" [1037] \"Well, yes, we went in and out all morning,\" Bill said, \"But we didn't hear any noise, and we didn't see anything happen.\" [1038] \"If we had been there in the afternoon, we might have heard the screams,\" Ted said regretfully, \"What a terrible scream it was!\" [1039] \"Do you know Miss Pemshaw, the owner of that house?\" [1040] The two children looked at each other and then nodded. [1041] \"She's blind,\" Ted said. \"But she can walk around the garden freely without any help from a cane or anything. Once she threw a ball back to us, and she threw it straight.\" [1042] \"You didn't see her at all yesterday?\" [1043] The children shook their heads. [1044] \"We don't see her in the morning. She's always out. After tea, she usually comes out to the garden.\" [1045] Colin was examining a hosepipe that ran from the tap in the room to a corner near the pear tree in the garden. [1046] \"I never knew pear trees needed watering,\" he said. [1047] \"Oh, that,\" Bill looked a bit embarrassed. [1048] \"If you didn't,\" Colin said, \"if you climbed up this tree,\" he looked at the two children and suddenly grinned, \"you could squirt the cat with water, right?\" [1049] The two children shuffled their feet on the ground, looking around, not looking at Colin. [1050] \"You did that, didn't you?\" Colin said. [1051] \"Oh, this,\" said Bill, \"won't hurt them. The hose won't, \"he said innocently, \"like a slingshot.\" [1052] \"I suppose you've played with a slingshot before.\" [1053] \"Not really,\" said Ted, \"we've never hit anything with it.\" [1054] \"Well, that hose must have brought you a lot of joy, anyway,\" said Colin, \"and then Mrs. Hamm would come over and complain, right?\" [1055] \"She's always complaining,\" said Bill. [1056] \"Have you ever jumped over her fence?\" [1057] \"There's barbed wire on this side, we can't get through,\" Ted said frankly. [1058] \"But you did get into her garden, right? How did you do that?\" [1059] \"Well, you can walk through the fence and into Miss Pemshaw's garden. Then you follow a path to the right, and you can push through the fence into Mrs. Hamm's garden. There's a hole in the barbed wire.\" [1060] \"Shut up, you idiot!\" Bill said. [1061] \"I bet you've been looking for clues ever since the murder, haven't you?\" Huckleberry said. [1062] The two children exchanged glances. [1063] \"After seeing the movie, I heard about the murder, and I'll bet you went over the fence to the garden at number 19 and had a good look around, didn't you?\" [1064] \"Uh-oh...\" Bill hesitated cautiously. [1065] \"That's how it always happens.\" Huckleberry said seriously. \"You may have found something we missed. If you, uh, picked it up and could show it to me, I would be grateful.\" [1066] Bill made up his mind. [1067] \"Get it for them, Ted,\" he said. [1068] Ted obediently ran off. [1069] \"I'm afraid we don't have anything particularly good,\" Bill admitted. \"We just pretend they're important.\" [1070] He nervously looked at Hakast. [1071] \"I understand that,\" Hakast said. \"That's the way police work often is. There's a lot of disappointment.\" [1072] Bill looked relieved. [1073] Ted ran back. He handed over a dirty, knotted handkerchief that jingled as he unwrapped it. Hakast untied the knot and spread out the contents, with the two children standing on either side of him. [1074] There was a handle that had fallen off a cup, a shard of pottery in the shape of a willow branch, a broken sponge, a rusty dinner fork, a coin, a clothespin, a piece of colored glass, and half a pair of scissors. [1075] \"Some interesting things,\" the detective said gravely. [1076] He looked sympathetically at the eager faces of the children and picked up the piece of glass casually. [1077] \"I'll take this. It might be connected to something.\" [1078] Colin picked up the coin and examined it closely. [1079] \"This isn't British currency,\" Ted said. [1080] \"No,\" Colin said. \"It's not British.\u201d He looked at Hakaste. \"We might take this too,\" he suggested. [1081] \"Don't tell anyone.\" Hakast said mysteriously. [1082] The children happily agreed. [1083] Chapter 11\n[1084] \"Lymes.\" Colin thought. [1085] \"What about him?\" [1086] \"I like his name, that's all. He's often abroad, which is worth noting. His wife says he's an engineer, but that's all she knows about him.\" [1087] \"She's a good woman.\" Hakaste said. [1088] \"Yes, but not a happy woman.\" [1089] \"It's hard, that's all. Having children always makes things hard.\" [1090] \"I think it's more than that.\" [1091] \"I'm sure the person you're looking for wouldn't be the kind to bring his family along,\" Hakaste said skeptically. [1092] \"You wouldn't know,\" Colin said. \"How useful it is to have children as a cover. You'd be surprised if you knew. A poor widow with several children would be very willing to be taken care of.\" [1093] \"I don't think she's that kind of person,\" Hakaste said seriously. [1094] \"I don't mean living in sin, my dear friend. I mean she agreed to be known as Mrs. Rimmers and even provided her background information. Of course, he spun a clever story for her. Something like, he was doing spy work for us, for example. All because of strong patriotic spirit.\" [1095] Hakaste shook his head. [1096] \"You live in a strange world, Colin,\" he said. [1097] \"Yes, we do. I suppose, you know, that someday I'll have to leave... There are people who have forgotten right from wrong. Half of them work for both purposes, but in the end, they don't even know their true stance. Standards can make things confusing. Anyway, let's get back to the point.\" [1098] \"We should pay a visit to the MacNorton house,\" Hakast said, stopping at the gate of 63. \"His garden has a small corner connected to 19, just like the Brandes.\" [1099] \"What do you know about MacNorton?\" [1100] \"Not much. They moved here a year ago. An old couple, both retired professors. Mr. MacNorton is a gardening enthusiast.\" [1101] In front of the garden was a rose bush, and in the flower bed under the window, narcissus bloomed. [1102] A young woman with a joyful face and wearing a brightly printed apron opened the door for them,\n[1103] \"Is it you who's knocking? What's going on?\" [1104] Hakast whispered, \"Finally, we found a house with foreigners.\" Then he handed her a business card. [1105] \"Police!\" the young woman said, taking a step back and staring at Hakast as if he were a demon. [1106] \"Is Mrs. McDonough at home?\" Hakaste asked. [1107] \"Mrs. McDonough is over here.\" [1108] She led them into the living room, from which you could overlook the back garden. The living room was empty. [1109] \"She's upstairs.\" The young woman stopped smiling. She walked into the hallway and then called, \"Mrs. McDonough, Mrs. McDonough.\" [1110] A voice came from afar, \"Hmm. What is it, Greta?\" [1111] \"The police are here. Two policemen. I've made them wait in the living room.\" [1112] Footsteps sounded from upstairs, \"Oh, dear. Oh, dear, what now?\" There were more footsteps, 