e to check on other things, then came back inside and spoke to Miss Pemarthy in the kitchen, telling her I had to take these clocks and would give her a receipt.\" [597] \"I remember. I heard you say that.\" [598] \"I then told the lady I would arrange for her to be driven home, and I asked you to take her to the car.\" [599] \"Yes.\" [600] \"Even though Miss Pemarthy said she didn't need a receipt, as the clocks weren't hers, I still gave her one. Then I came to find you. I told Edward to carefully pack the clocks in the living room, and bring them here. All the clocks except the cuckoo clock were packed. Of course, there was also the grandfather clock. That's where I made my mistake. I should have been very specific and told them there were four clocks. Edward said he went in immediately and did what I said. But he insisted that there were only three clocks, besides the two fixed ones.\" [601] \"There wasn't much time between all of this,\" I said, \"which means\u2014\"\n[602] \"It could have been Pemahux. She might have wrapped the clock up and taken it directly to the kitchen after I left the house.\" [603] \"But what about the motive?\" [604] \"We need more information. Was there anyone else? Was it the girl who did it?\" [605] I thought for a moment. \"I don't think so. I\u2014\" I stopped suddenly, remembering something. [606] \"So she might have,\" Hakaste said, \"continue. When was this?\" [607] \"Just as we came out, walking towards the police car,\" I said glumly, \"she forgot her gloves. I said 'I'll go get them for you,' but she said 'Oh, I just know where they are. I don't mind going back to the house now, since the body's been moved.' And then she ran back to the house. But she only left for a minute\u2014\"\n[608] \"Did she have her gloves on when she came back to you, or was she holding them?\" [609] I hesitated. \"Yes, yes, I think she was holding them.\" [610] \"Obviously she wasn't,\" Hakaste said, \"otherwise you wouldn't have hesitated.\" [611]