ound a job at the Allenberg Institute.\" [646] \"Maybe she has a mental illness?\" [647] \"Because of her obsession with clocks and insurance agents?\" [648] \"It's simply indescribable, too strange. My strong curiosity makes me say this, 'like Ariadne Oliver at her worst, or the late Gary Gregson at the height of his career...'\"\n[649] \"Go on and express your emotions. You're not the poor wretch in charge of the criminal investigation team. You don't have to flatter the senior police officers or the police commissioner, or anyone else.\" [650] \"Okay! Maybe we can find useful clues from the neighbors.\" [651] \"No telling,\" Hercule Poirot said coldly, \"If the man was killed in the front garden and then the two masked men carried him into the house. No one looked out the window or noticed anything. This isn't the countryside, our luck would be terrible. Wilbraham Crescent is a residential area for the upper class. The women who finish their work at one o'clock are already home. There's not even a handcart on the street \u2013\"\n[652] \"No elderly people sitting by the window all day, sickly?\" [653] \"We wish there were, but that's impossible.\" [654] \"What about number 18 or 20?\" [655] \"Number 18 is occupied by Mr. Waitehouse \u2013 an assistant to Messrs. Gastford and Swinburne, the lawyers, and his sister who comes to take care of him in his spare time. As for number 20, I only know that a woman lives there who owns eighteen cats. I don't like cats \u2013\"\n[656] I told him, being a policeman is no easy task. And then we set off. [657] Chapter 7\n[658] Mr. Walters paced up and down the steps of 18 Wilbraham Crescent, anxiously looking back at his sister. [659] \"Are you sure you're right?\" Mr. Walters said. [660] Miss Walters huffed in anger. [661] \"I really don't understand what you mean, James.\" [662] Mr. Waltershouse looked apologetic. He must have often expressed regret, as his face seemed to bear the traces of such an expression. [663] \"Well, I just thought, dear, of what happened next door yesterday\u2026\"\n[664] Mr. Waltershouse was preparing to go to his law firm. He was a gentlemanly man with gray hair, slightly hunched, and pale complexion, not pink, but not sickly looking either. [665] Miss Waltershouse was tall and slender, a woman who was serious and intolerant of anyone who was not serious. [666] \"James, just because someone was murdered next door, does that mean I have a reason to be murdered today?\" [667] \"Well, Edith,\" said Mr. Warthouse, \"it depends on the kind of person the killer is?\" [668] \"Do you really think, James, that someone was wandering up and down Wilbraham Crescent, looking for a house to murder someone in? It's sacrilege.\" [669] \"Sacrilege, Edith?\" Mr. Warthouse was astonished. He would never dare say such a thing. [670] \"The Passover memories,\" said Miss Warthouse, \"remind you that it comes from the Bible.\" [671] \"I think that's a bit of a stretch, Edith,\" said Mr. Walterhouse. [672] \"I should have been thinking about who might come here to try and murder me,\" said Miss Walterhouse, becoming quite agitated. [673] Her brother pondered silently, the idea of someone trying to murder her seemed quite impossible. If he were choosing a victim, he would never choose his sister. If someone did attempt it, the assailant would likely be beaten back by a poker or door latch, covered in blood, and handed over to the police in such a humiliating and unusual circumstance. [674] \"I just mean,\" he said, looking more apologetic, \"yes, there have been some unsavory characters around here.\" [675] \"We know very little about what's been happening,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"Rumors are spreading all over the place. This morning, Mrs. Black said some strange things.\" [676] \"I hope so,\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch. He had no intention of listening to the idle gossip from the maids. His sister never wasted time debunking such sensational stories; she only took a moment of amusement from them. [677] \"Some people say,\" Miss Watrous said, \"that this man was an accountant or a trustee at Allerton College, and he came to Miss Pembroke to inquire about some discrepancies in the accounts.\" [678] \"And Miss Pembroke killed him?\" Mr. Watrous seemed amused. \"A blind woman? Are you sure\u2014\"\n[679] \"She quietly wrapped a wire around his neck and strangled him,\" Miss Watrous said. \"He didn't suspect anything, you know. Who would suspect a blind woman? I don't believe it,\" she continued. \"I'm sure Miss Pembroke is a woman of excellent character. We may disagree on many things, but that doesn't mean I consider her a suspect. I just think her views are narrow and stubborn. There are other issues besides education. All these new grammar schools, they look so peculiar, with classrooms made of glass. You might think they were built for growing cucumbers or tomatoes. I'm convinced they're not suitable for children during the summer months. Mrs. Hazes told me herself that her daughter Susan doesn't like the new classrooms at the school. She said because of these glass windows, you always want to look outside, and you can't concentrate on your studies.\" [680] \"Oh, dear!\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch again. \"Well, I'm afraid I'll be late. Goodbye, dear. Take care of yourself. Remember to lock the door!\" [681] Miss Watrous sniffed disdainfully as her brother left. She closed the door and started up the stairs. She paused to think, walked over to her golf bag, and took out a nine-iron, placing it in a prominent spot near the front door. \"Well,\" Miss Watrous said, satisfied, \"James was just talking nonsense. But it's always better to be prepared. These days, patients are released from mental institutions too easily, encouraged to lead normal lives, and I think it's dangerous for innocent people.\" [682] As Mrs. Hazard hurried up the stairs, Miss Watrous was in her bedroom. Mrs. Hazard was short and plump, like a rubber ball. She enjoyed every big or small event in life. [683] \"There are some gentlemen who want to see you,\" Mrs. Hazard said urgently. \"But,\" she added, \"they're not real gentlemen. They're policemen.\" [684] She handed her the card. [685] \"Inspector Hatch,\" Miss Watrous said. \"Did you bring them to the living room?\" [686] \"No. I left them in the dining room. I've cleared away the breakfast things, and I thought that would be more suitable for them. I mean, they're just policemen.\" [687] Miss Watkins was not very pleased with this reason, but she said, \"I'll go down.\" [688] \"I think they're here to ask you about Miss Pemberton,\" Mrs. Hazle said. \"To see if you've noticed any funny behavior. They say these fits can come on suddenly, with no warning. But usually you can tell by the way they talk, by the way they look in the eyes. But for a blind woman, that's impossible, isn't it? Er\u2014\" She shook her head. [689] Miss Watkins descended the stairs, with a slight air of joyful curiosity, which was really her usual combative spirit, entering the dining room. [690] \"Is it Detective Chief Inspector Hakast?\" [691] \"Good morning, Miss Walterhouse,\" said Huckleberry, rising. Miss Walterhouse didn't even acknowledge the tall, dark-skinned young man who had come with him. She didn't even notice the whispered \"Constable Lamb\" that followed. [692] \"I hope I'm not disturbing you too early,\" said Huckleberry, \"but I thought you'd know why we're here. You've heard about what happened next door yesterday, haven't you?\" [693] \"A murder in my own backyard is never ignored,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"I've even turned away a few reporters who came asking questions.\" [694] \"You turned them away?\" [695] \"Of course.\" [696] \"You did the right thing,\" Hakaste said. \"Of course, they like to sneak back in, but I'm sure you can handle it with your abilities.\" [697] For such praise, Miss Houseworth allowed herself a hint of pleasure. [698] \"I hope you don't mind us asking you the same questions,\" Hakaste said. \"But if you saw anything that could be helpful to us, I'm sure we would be grateful for your information. At that time, I think you were in the house?\" [699] \"I don't know when the murder happened,\" Miss Houseworth said. [700] \"We think it was between 1:30 and 2:30 PM.\" [701] \"I was in the house, yes, indeed.\" [702] \"Where was your brother?\" [703] \"He doesn't come for lunch. Who was murdered? The local papers haven't mentioned anything.\" [704] \"A stranger?\" [705] \"It seems so now.\" [706] \"You're not telling me that for Miss Pemerton he's a stranger too?\" [707] \"Miss Pemerton told us very confidently that she had no appointment with this particular guest, and she didn't know who he was.\" [708] \"She couldn't be so sure,\" said Miss Watrous. \"She can't see.\" [709] \"We described the person's appearance to her in great detail.\" [710] \"What did he look like?\" [711] Mr. Huckleberry took a photograph from the envelope and handed it to her. [712] \"That's the man,\" he said. \"Do you recognize him?\" [713] Miss Warton looked at the photo. \"No, no... I'm sure I've never seen him before. Oh dear. He looks like a very respectable person.\" [714] \"He does look respectable,\" said the detective. \"He looks like a lawyer or a businessman.\" [715] \"Yes, that's true. There's nothing about this photo that suggests any misfortune. He looks like he's sleeping.\" [716] Hakast didn't tell her that this photo was chosen from among many others, the one that was least likely to cause discomfort. [717] \"Death can sometimes be a peaceful thing,\" he said. \"I think this particular man never expected what was about to happen next.\" [718] \"What did Miss Pembroke have to say about this?\" Miss Walters asked. [719] \"She was very surprised.\" [720] \"That's strange!\" Miss Walters commented. [721] \"Now, please help us out, Miss Walterhouse. If you can remember yesterday, try to think if you saw anything outside the window, or if you happened to be in your garden between twelve-thirty and three o'clock, tell us what happened during that time?\" [722] Miss Walterhouse recalled. [723] \"Yes, I was in the garden... let me think. It should have been before one o'clock. I went into the house from the garden at a quarter to one, washed my hands, and then sat down to lunch.\" [724] \"Did you see Miss Pemahku enter or leave the house?\" [725] \"I think she came back. I heard the creak of the gate. Yes, usually after twelve-thirty.\" [726] \"Did you speak to her?\" [727] \"Oh, no. It was just the sound of the gate that made me look up. That's when she usually comes home. I think she finished her class.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 7:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nIs this the man who came to ask me if I wanted to buy a set of fourteen-volume new encyclopedias? Or the one who came with the latest vacuum cleaner? We have no connection with him. He went to the front garden, where he annoyed my husband, who was planting bulbs. You know, he didn't want to be disturbed, but the man kept talking about his product. You know, how it comes up and down to clean curtains, how it cleans doorsteps, stairs, cushions, and does a thorough cleaning. He almost mentioned everything. Then Angus looked up at him and said, 'Can it plant bulbs?' I must say it made me laugh out loud, as it forced the man to suddenly step back and leave.\" [1120] \"So, you're absolutely sure this man is the one in the photo?\" [1121] \"Well, no, I can't be sure,\" said Mrs. MacNutt, \"because it was a younger man. Let me think. But anyway, I think I've seen this face before. Yes. The more I look at it, the more sure I am that he came here and made me buy something.\" [1122] \"Maybe insurance?\" [1123] \"No, no, not insurance. My husband took care of all that. We are fully insured. No. But anyway, yes, the more I look at this photo\u2026\"\n[1124] Hackett was not encouraged by this, on the contrary, perhaps even worse. From his rich experience, he thought Mrs. MacNutt was too eager to have seen someone related to the murder case. So the longer she looked at the photo, the more certain she would be that she could remember the man in the photo. [1125] He sighed. [1126] \"I think he was driving a big truck,\" said Mrs. McNutt, \"but I can't remember when I saw him. I think it was a bakery truck.\" [1127] \"Did you see him yesterday, Mrs. McNutt?\" [1128] Mrs. McNutt's face fell. She brushed her greasy gray curls back from her forehead. [1129] \"No, no, not yesterday,\" she said, \"at least -\" She stopped, \"I think not.\" Then her face brightened again. \"Maybe my husband will remember.\" [1130] \"Is he home?\" [1131] \"Oh, he's out in the garden,\" she said, pointing out the window. An older man was pushing a wheelbarrow along a path. [1132] \"Perhaps we should go out and talk to him.\" [1133] \"Of course, come this way.\" [1134] She led them through a side door and into the garden. Mr. MacNorton looked flushed and sweaty. [1135] \"These two gentlemen are from the police station, Angus,\" his wife panted. \"They want to ask about the murder at Miss Pemmican's house. Here's a picture of the deceased. Do you know, I'm sure I've seen him before. Was it last week when he came asking if we had any antiques to dispose of? Was it that man?\" [1136] \"Let me see,\" Mr. McNaughton said, \"just hold it up for me. I've got mud on my hands and can't touch anything.\" He addressed Mr. Harkness, \"You know about my gardening, don't you?\" [1137] \"Yes, Mr. Harkness said so,\" Mr. McNaughton grunted. [1138] \"Not Mrs. Lymes, I hope.\" [1139] \"No, it was Mr. Brand.\" [1140] Angus McNaughton snorted. [1141] \"Brand doesn't know what gardening is,\" he said, \"he just digs holes and then plants begonias, geraniums, and Cape tulips. That's not what I call gardening. Those flowers would survive in the park. Are you interested in shrubs, Inspector? Of course, it's not the season for planting shrubs, but I have a couple of shrubs here that you might be surprised to see I've kept alive. It's said that shrubs thrive best in Devonshire and Cornwall.\" [1142] \"I'm afraid I'm not much of a gardener,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1143] Mr. McNaughton looked at him as an artist might look at someone who doesn't understand art but knows what they like. [1144] \"Perhaps I should start talking about the unpleasant topic,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1145] \"Certainly. It happened yesterday, I was in the garden.\" [1146] \"Really?\" [1147] \"Well, I mean, when I heard that girl's scream, I was here.\" [1148] \"What were you doing?\" [1149] \"Well,\" Mr. MacNoughton said, startled, \"I didn't do anything. In fact, I thought it was those two noisy kids from the Lymeses' house. They're always yelling, screaming, making a racket.\" [1150] \"But the scream came from a different direction, didn't it?\" [1151] \"Yes, if those boys had stayed in their own garden. But they don't, you know. They're always jumping over other people's fences and hedges. They're chasing poor Mrs. Black's cats all over the place. Nobody's watching them, that's the trouble. Their mother's a nice, quiet sort. Of course, when there's no man in the house, the children get out of hand.\" [1152] \"I know Mr. Rimmers often goes abroad.\" [1153] \"He's an engineer, as far as I know,\" Mr. McNaughton said vaguely, \"always on the move. Dam building, or pipelines, or something like that. I'm not criticizing, dear, but I mean this kind of dam building, or laying oil pipelines, or whatever. I don't know the real story. He rushed off to Sweden a month ago, leaving a lot of work for the children's mother\u2014cooking and housework. Well, no wonder they're getting wild. They're not bad at heart, they just need discipline.\" [1154] \"Did you see anything besides hearing the scream? I mean, when did you hear it, by the way?\" [1155] \"I'm not sure. I usually take off my watch when I go into the garden. The other day, the water pipe was broken, and it took me a while to fix it. What time was that, dear? You heard it too, didn't you?\" [1156] \"Probably around half past two, at least half an hour after we had lunch.\" [1157] \"I see. When do you have lunch?\" [1158] \"At half past one,\" Mr. McNaughton said. \"If we're lucky. Our Danish girl doesn't have a good sense of time.\" [1159] \"And then what? Do you take a nap?\" [1160] \"Sometimes. But not today. I need to finish what I started. I cleared up a lot of waste and added it to the compost pile.\" [1161] \"Great, making compost,\" said Hakaste seriously. [1162] Mr. McNuton immediately brightened up. [1163] \"Absolutely right. Nothing beats homemade compost. I've convinced many people of that. Using chemical fertilizers is like self-destruction, isn't it? Let me show you.\" [1164] He enthusiastically pulled Hakaste's arm and pushed the wheelbarrow along the path to the fence separating his garden from No. 19. The compost was surrounded by lilacs, making it look enticing. Mr. McNuton pushed the wheelbarrow to a small shed next to it. The shed was neatly arranged with various tools. [1165] \"You've organized everything so well,\" commented Hakaste. [1166] \"You must take care of your tools,\" McNotton said. [1167] Hakaste closely observed the 19th garden. On the other side of the fence was a path lined with roses, leading to the corner of the house. [1168] \"Did you see anyone in the 19th garden or peering out the window while you were making compost?\" [1169] McNotton shook his head. [1170] \"I didn't see anyone. Sorry I couldn't help you, Detective.\" [1171] \"You know, Angus,\" his wife said, \"I'm sure I saw a shadowy figure hiding in the garden of number 19.\" [1172] \"I don't think you did, dear,\" her husband said firmly. \"I didn't either.\" [1173] \"That woman kept saying she saw this and that, as if she could spot anything,\" Hakaste grumbled as they got back in the car. [1174] \"Do you think she recognized the person in the photo?\" [1175] Hakaste shook his head. \"I doubt it. She's just imagining she's seen him. I know this kind of witness. When I asked her to clarify, she couldn't answer, right?\" [1176] \"That's right.\" [1177] \"Of course, she could have said she saw him on the bus or somewhere else, sitting across from her. But if you ask me, I think it's just her wishful thinking. What do you think?\" [1178] \"I agree.\" [1179] \"We didn't learn anything,\" Hakast sighed. \"Of course, some things seem strange. For example, Mrs. Black, no matter how she tried to associate herself with the cat, shouldn't be completely unaware of her neighbor Miss Pema, which seems impossible. And she was so confused about the murder case, showing no concern.\" [1180] \"She's just a muddled woman.\" [1181] \"No brains!\" Hakaste said. \"When you encounter such a silly woman, fires, thefts, and murders happening around them, they won't even pay attention.\" [1182] \"Her wire mesh fence is quite effective. The Victorian shrubs almost hide everything from view.\" [1183] They returned to the police station. Hakaste grinned at his friend:\n[1184] \"Well, Detective Blue, I can now let you go home.\" [1185] \"No more interviews?\" [1186] \"Not for now. I'll be visiting a few more places later, but not with you.\" [1187] \"All right, then. Thank you for this morning. Will you have my notes typed up? Here's the folder.\" \"You said the interrogation is tomorrow, isn't it? What time?\" [1188] \"Eleven o'clock.\" [1189] \"Good. I'll be back then.\" [1190] \"You're leaving?\" [1191] \"I'm going to London to give a report tomorrow. I can guess who it is.\" [1192] \"Don't say anything.\" [1193] Hakaste grinned. [1194] \"Say hello to that guy for me.\" [1195] \"Also, I might go see an expert,\" said Colin. [1196] \"An expert? What kind of expert? What's going on?\" [1197] \"Nothing, just stupid. Not that kind of expert. He's in your line of work. He's my father's friend, and he's my friend. You know, he loves these kind of things. It excites him. I think he needs a little stimulation.\" [1198] \"Scotland Yard?\" [1199] \"No. A private detective, a friend of my father's, and also a friend of mine. Your bizarre work would be right up his alley. He enjoys these things. It would excite him. I think he needs some stimulation.\" [1200] \"What's his name?\" [1201] \"Hercule Poirot.\" [1202] \"I've heard of him. I thought he was dead.\" [1203] \"He's still alive. But I feel he has nothing to do. It's worse than being dead.\" [1204] \"You're a strange guy, Colin. Having such strange friends. \" \"And you're no different,\" Colin said with a grin. [1205] ***\n[1206] [1] In English, the words \"dam\" (as in a dam) and \"damn\" are pronounced the same. [1207] Chapter 12\n[1208] After sending Colin away, Inspector Hercule looked at the neatly written address on his notepad and nodded. Then he quietly put the notebook back in his pocket and began to deal with the pile of daily files on his desk. [1209] He worked all day. He sent someone out to buy him coffee and sandwiches, received a report from Sergeant Grey - no useful leads were found. No one recognized Mr. Kuri's photo at the train station or bus station. The check of the clothing in the laboratory was also fruitless. The suit was made by a skilled tailor, but the label of the tailor's shop had been cut off. Was the information that Mr. Kuri wanted to hide his identity or the identity of the killer? The detailed report on the deceased's teeth had been distributed to various places, which was likely the most useful clue. It would take some time, but eventually, there would be a result. Unless, Mr. Kuri was a foreigner? Hercule imagined, it was likely that the deceased was French, but his clothes were not French-made, and there were no laundry shop labels to inquire about. [1210] Inspector Hercule was not impatient. Confirming the identity is often a slow process. But in the end, someone would come forward to confirm it, perhaps the laundry shop owner, the dentist, the doctor, or the landlady. Mr. Kuri's photo would be sent to various police stations and would be published in newspapers. Soon, Mr. Kuri's true identity would be discovered. [1211] Meanwhile, there was other work to be done, not just Mr. Kirby's case. Detective Hakast worked non-stop until 5:30 PM. He glanced at his watch again and thought it was a suitable time to visit a few more places. [1212] According to Inspector Gray's report, Ms. Sheila Webber had returned to Cavenish Typewriting Agency and had an appointment with Professor Puddy at the Heron Hotel at 5:00 PM, and she would not leave until after 6:00 PM. [1213] What was the name of the aunt? Mrs. Rotten, Mrs. Rotten. Living at 14 Palmerston Road. He did not drive his police car but chose to walk the shortcut. [1214] It was said that Palmerston Road used to be a lifeless road. Detective Hakast noticed that most of the houses had been converted into apartments or small houses. As he turned the corner, a girl was walking along the sidewalk towards him. She seemed to hesitate for a moment. Detective Hakast was thinking about other things at the time, and he thought she was going to ask him for directions. But if that was the case, why did she change her mind and continue to walk past him? He wondered why he suddenly thought of shoes. Shoes... No, just one shoe. This girl made him feel vaguely familiar. Who was she? Someone he had seen recently... maybe she recognized him and wanted to talk to him? [1215] He stopped and looked back at her. She was walking quickly by now. The problem was, her face had no clear and distinct features, making it hard to remember, unless there was a special reason. She had blue eyes, fair skin, and a slightly open mouth. The mouth. This made him think of something else. What was she doing with her mouth? Speaking? Putting on lipstick? No. He was a bit annoyed with himself. Detective Hakast prided himself on being able to remember people's facial features. He could say that he would never forget the people he had seen in the defendant's and witness's stands, but after all, he didn't just deal with those people. He couldn't remember, for example, every waitress who had served him. He couldn't remember every bus conductor. So he decided not to think about it for now. [1216] He arrives at number 14. The door is half open, and there are four bells beside it with names written underneath. He sees Mrs. Rudd. She lives in the ground-floor apartment. He goes in and rings the doorbell on the left of the hall. After a few minutes, he hears movement. Soon, footsteps are heard from inside. A tall, thin woman opens the door, her black hair disheveled, wearing a large apron, and seems to be out of breath. There is an onion smell coming from the house, obviously from the kitchen. [1217] \"Is this Mrs. Rudd?\" [1218] \"What do you want?\" She looks at him suspiciously and seems a little annoyed. [1219] He thinks she is about forty-five years old and looks a bit like a gypsy. [1220] \"What do you want?\" [1221] \"Could I trouble you for a moment to discuss something?\" [1222] \"Well, what is it? I'm very busy now.\" She spoke quickly, \"Are you a reporter?\" [1223] \"Of course not,\" Detective Hackett said sympathetically, \"I imagine you've been bothered by reporters quite a bit, haven't you?\" [1224] \"Yes, they keep knocking on the door and ringing the bell, asking all sorts of stupid questions.\" [1225] \"That can be quite irritating, I know,\" Detective Hackett said, \"I hope we can help alleviate some of that for you, Mrs. Rutter. I'm Detective Hackett, by the way, in charge of the case that has reporters bothering you. We'll do our best to prevent that from happening, but unfortunately, we can't control the press, you know, they have their own rights.\" [1226] \"It's inconsiderate of them to bother others like that,\" Mrs. Rudd said, \"claiming they have to report the news for the public. I've seen their reports in the newspaper, and they're all full of nonsense. As far as I know, they just make up facts. Please come in, Detective.\" [1227] She stepped back a few steps, and Detective Hakast walked through the doorway. She closed the door. There were several envelopes on the doormat. Mrs. Rudd was about to bend down to pick them up when Detective Hakast politely beat her to it. He glanced at the envelopes, checking the addresses, before handing them over to the hostess. [1228] \"Thank you.\" [1229] She placed them on the table in the hall. [1230] \"Go ahead to the living room, please? I'll be right there. The food in the kitchen is almost ready.\" [1231] She hurried off to the kitchen. Detective Hackett glanced one last time at the letters on the hall table. One was addressed to Mrs. Rotten, and the other two were for Miss R.S. Webber. He followed Mrs. Rotten's directions into the room. It was a small, messy room with old, stained furniture and unidentifiable stains and spots everywhere. There was an eye-catching, expensive Venetian cast glass dish, an abstract model, two colorful velvet cushions, and an exotic-looking large ceramic shallow dish. He thought that either the aunt or the niece had some taste. [1232] Mrs. Rotten returned, panting even more than before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe exact time will be recorded at Somerset House. Where did you get married? Do you remember the location?\" he asked. [2263] \"In a place called Dunbrooke, St. Michael's Church, I think that's the name of the church. I really don't want to think that this happened over twenty years ago. It would make me feel like I have one foot in the grave,\" Mrs. Rival said. [2264] She stood up and extended her hand. Hakaste shook her hand and bid her farewell. He returned to the table, sat down, and tapped the table with his pencil. Soon after, Detective Inspector Gray entered. [2265] \"Satisfied?\" he asked. [2266] \"Seems so, detective,\" said Poirot. \"The name is Harry Castleton, which may be an alias. We'll see what we can find out about this man. It seems that more than one woman is out to get him for revenge.\" [2267] \"He looks quite respectable,\" said Grey. [2268] \"That's just his modus operandi,\" said Poirot. [2269] He began to ponder the clock with the inscription \"Rosemary.\" Was it a souvenir? [2270] Chapter XXII\n[2271] Narrated by Colin Lamb\n[2272] 1\n[2273] \"You're back,\" said Hercule Poirot. [2274] He carefully placed the bookmark in the book. This time, there was a cup of hot coffee on the table next to him. Poirot's taste in beverages was always a mystery! But this time, he didn't offer to share. [2275] \"How are you?\" I asked. [2276] \"Too noisy, too noisy. They're renovating, redecorating, and the whole structure of these apartments seems to have changed.\u201d\n[2277] \"Won't they improve it?\" [2278] \"I suppose so, but for me, it's the most irritating. I have to rearrange my schedule. There's even the smell of paint!\" [2279] He waved his hand, as if trying to brush away these troubles, and asked,\n[2280] \"How's your work going, then?\" [2281] I slowly said, \"I don't know.\" [2282] \"Oh, there may be such a situation.\" [2283] \"I found what they wanted me to find. But I didn't find that man. I don't know what they want. Intelligence? Or a corpse?\" [2284] \"Speaking of corpses, I read the statement from the delayed trial in Crocetta. It said someone was murdered intentionally. The name of the deceased has finally been found out.\" [2285] I nodded. [2286] \"Who is Harry Castleton?\" [2287] \"His wife identified him. Have you been to Crofton?\" [2288] \"Not yet. I'm planning to go tomorrow.\" [2289] \"Oh, do you have any free time?\" [2290] \"No. I'm still busy with work. My work keeps me \u2013\" I paused and continued, \"During my time abroad, I had no idea what was happening. Is it just someone claiming the body? What do you think about this?\" [2291] Poirot shrugged. [2292] \"It's still under investigation.\" [2293] \"Yes, the police have been doing a good job\u2014\"\n[2294] \"Mostly because the wife is very cooperative.\" [2295] \"Mrs. Melina Rival! A nice name!\" [2296] \"This reminds me of something,\" said Poirot. \"What could it be?\" [2297] He looked at me thoughtfully, but I couldn't help him. I knew Poirot, and the name probably reminded him of something. [2298] \"I visited a friend at a country house,\" Poirot mused, then shook his head. \"No, that was a long time ago.\" [2299] \"When I get back to London, I'll come and tell you everything I find out about Mrs. Melina Rival from Hercule,\" I promised him. [2300] Poirot waved his hand and said, \"That won't be necessary.\" [2301] \"You mean you already know everything about her?\" I asked. [2302] \"No, I mean I'm not interested in her\u2014\"\n[2303] \"You're not interested? I don't understand,\" I shook my head. [2304] \"Focus on the essential elements. Now, tell me about the girl named Ina\u2014the one who died in the phone booth on Wilbraham Crescent.\" [2305] \"I've told you everything I know about her. I have no more information on the girl.\" [2306] \"So, everything you know is, or can tell me is, that you saw the girl at the print shop looking like a pitiful little white rabbit, her heel stuck in the grille, and she looked angry\u2014\" He suddenly stopped talking. \"Where was that grille, by the way?\" [2307] \"How would I know, Polo?\" [2308] \"If you asked, you would know. How can you learn details if you don't ask more questions at the right time?\" [2309] \"But knowing where her heel fell off, what difference does that make?\" [2310] \"Perhaps it doesn't matter. In other words, we should know where the girl was at that time, which may be related to the people she saw or the events that happened there.\" [2311] \"You're just making things up. Anyway, I do know it was near the office. She said she bought a round loaf and then limped back to the office in her stockings. She ate the bread there and said she was wondering how she would get home.\" [2312] \"Oh, so how did she get home?\" Poirot asked with interest. [2313] \"I don't know.\" [2314] \"Oh, that's impossible. You'll never get to the bottom of things like this. That's why you only learn irrelevant information.\" [2315] \"You'd better go to Croisset yourself and ask those questions.\" I said angrily. [2316] \"I can't now. Next week there's an auction of a very interesting author's manuscript\u2014\"\n[2317] \"Still your obsession?\" [2318] \"Yes, indeed.\u201d His eyes sparkled. \"Buy John Dickson Carr's works, or as he sometimes called himself, Carter Dickson\u2014\"\n[2319] I left him before he could start, pretending to be rushing to an important appointment. I didn't have the patience to listen to him enumerate the past masters of detective novels. [2320] 2\n[2321] The next evening, I sat on the steps of Harkness's house, and when he came back, I felt as if I had suddenly come out of a depression. [2322] \"Hey, Colin? Is that you? You just popped up again, right?\" [2323] \"It would be more pleasant if you disappeared for good.\" [2324] \"How long have you been here, sitting on my house steps?\" [2325] \"Oh, about half an hour.\" [2326] \"I'm sorry I couldn't let you in.\" [2327] \"I could have easily gone in myself,\" I said angrily, \"but you don't know what training we've had!\" [2328] \"Why didn't you go in then?\" [2329] \"I didn't want to tarnish your reputation,\" I explained, \"if a detective's house was so easily broken into, it would be embarrassing for you.\" [2330] Hakast took out a key from his pocket and opened the door. [2331] \"Come in,\" he said, \"stop making excuses.\" [2332] He walked towards the living room to prepare some drinks. [2333] \"When did you get back?\" [2334] I said not long ago, and we both took our drinks and sat down. [2335] \"We've made some progress,\" Hakaste said, \"we've identified the victim.\" [2336] \"I saw the newspaper report. Who's Harry Castleton?\" [2337] \"A man who seemed respectable. He made a living by marrying or getting engaged to wealthy and gullible women who admired his financial expertise and entrusted their savings to him to manage. Then, he disappeared without a trace.\" [2338] \"He doesn't seem like that kind of person,\" I said, thinking back on it. [2339] \"That's exactly what made him successful.\" [2340] \"But hasn't he been charged with anything?\" [2341] \"No. We conducted an investigation, but it didn't go well. He often changes his name. Although they believe Harry Castleton, Raymond Blair, Lawrence Dalton, and Roger Byron are all the same person, they cannot prove it. Those women, you understand, won't say anything. They'd rather lose their money. This person's name changes constantly, suddenly appearing here or there, always profiting in the same way. It's too unbelievable. There were rumors that Roger Byron disappeared from the south, and a Lawrence Dalton was active near the River Tyne in Newcastle. He's shy about taking photos and always avoids having his girlfriend snap quick shots of him. So, these are events that happened a long time ago, around fifteen to twenty years ago. After that, he seemed to have truly disappeared. There were rumors that he had died, but some said he had gone abroad\u2014\"\n[2342] \"Regardless, there has been no news of him until he died on the carpet in Miss Pemaroo's living room?\" I said. [2343] \"Exactly.\" [2344] \"But there are many possible causes of his death.\" [2345] \"Indeed.\" [2346] \"Women never forget the insults they've suffered,\" I reminded him. [2347] \"You're right. Some women never forget\u2014\"\n[2348] \"If this woman later became blind, wouldn't that be adding insult to injury\u2014\"\n[2349] \"It's just speculation. There's nothing to confirm it.\" [2350] \"What did his wife look like? What was her name? Melina Rival? What an odd name! It couldn't be her name, could it?\" [2351] \"Her real name is Flora Gapp. She came up with it herself. It suited her at the time.\" [2352] \"What was she doing? A prostitute?\" [2353] \"Not exactly.\" [2354] \"What was she, past tense? A socialite, I believe.\" [2355] \"What I mean is that she was a good-natured woman, always there for her friends. She said she used to be an actress. Occasionally did some 'hostess' work. She was well-liked.\" [2356] \"Is all of that true?\" [2357] \"Quite credible. She was very certain when she identified the body. Not a moment's hesitation.\" [2358] \"That's lucky.\" [2359] \"Yes. At first, I was desperate. There were so many letters from wives! Then I began to think that a woman who could recognize her husband was a smart woman. Listen, I think Mrs. Rival knows more about her husband than she's letting on.\" [2360] \"Has she ever been involved in any criminal activities?\" [2361] \"No criminal record. I think she might have had some, maybe still has some, questionable friends. Nothing serious, just messing around, that sort of thing.\" [2362] \"What about the clocks?\" [2363] \"They don't mean anything to her. I think she's telling the truth. We traced the clocks' origins \u2013 the gilded clock and the Dresden porcelain clock were found at the Pombal Market [1]. It was no help at all! You should have seen the place on Saturday. The vendor thought an American girl bought them, but I think it's just a guess. There were so many American tourists at the Pombal Market. Her wife said a man bought them. But she couldn't remember what he looked like. The silver clock was bought from a silversmith in Bournemouth. A tall woman wanted to buy a birthday gift for her little daughter! She only remembers she was wearing a green hat.\" [2364] \"What about the fourth clock? The one that disappeared?\" [2365] \"That's none of your business.\" Huckleberry said. [2366] I knew what he meant. [2367] * * *\n[2368][1] Pimlico Market, located in the Notting Hill district, is one of the most famous outdoor markets in Britain, consisting of three different markets. The antique market at the southern end is the main destination for tourists to shop, and it is only open on Saturdays. [2369] Chapter 23\n[2370] Narrative by Colin Lamb\n[2371] I live in a small hotel near the police station. The barbecue is not bad, but that's about it. Otherwise, of course, it's very cheap. [2372] At ten o'clock in the morning, I called the Cavendish Typewriting Agency to say that I wanted a shorthand typist to type some letters and retype a commercial contract. My name is Douglas Weesby, and I live at the Clarendon Hotel (old hotels always have grand names). Is Miss Hilary Webber available? A friend of mine said she was excellent. [2373] I was lucky. Miss Hilary could come straight over. But she had an appointment at twelve. I said we could finish before then, as I had an appointment at twelve too. [2374] I waited at the Clarendon Hotel's revolving door, and Miss Hilary arrived. I went up to her. [2375] \"Mr. Douglas Weesby is at your service.\" I said. [2376] \"Did you make the call?\" [2377] \"Yes, I did.\" [2378] \"How could you do such a thing?\" She looks a bit angry. [2379] \"Why not? I am prepared to pay Cavendish Typewriting Agency. What difference does it make if we spend our precious time together at the Golden Daffodil Caf\u00e9 instead of dictating those boring letters? Let's go to a quiet place for a cup of coffee.\" [2380] The Golden Daffodil Caf\u00e9 truly lives up to its name, with its overall decor in bright and bold yellow. The Formica tabletop, plastic cushion, and cups and plates are all in a pale yellow. [2381] I ordered two coffees and scones. It was early, so the coffee shop was only the two of us. [2382] When the waitress took the order and left, we looked at each other across the table. [2383] \"How are you, Hilary?\" [2384] \"What do you mean? Am I okay?\" [2385] Dark circles under her eyes made her eyes appear lavender instead of light blue. \"You're not doing well, are you?\" [2386] \"Yes, no, I don't know. I thought you left.\" [2387] \"Yes. Just got back.\" [2388] \"Why?\" [2389] \"You know why.\" [2390] Her eyes lowered. (Note: This sentence is translated to convey the meaning of the original sentence, but the phrasing could be more natural in English, such as \"She lowered her eyes.\") [2391] \"I'm afraid of him.\" She didn't speak for at least a minute, which felt like an eternity to me. [2392] \"Who are you afraid of?\" [2393] \"Your friend, that detective. He thinks... he thinks I killed that man, thinks I killed Ina...\"\n[2394] \"Oh, that's just him, I reassured her. \"He always looks like he suspects everyone.\" [2395] \"No, Colin, it's not like that. Saying that doesn't help, I won't be happy. He's always believed I had something to do with this case from the start.\" [2396] \"My dear girl, there's no evidence against you. It's just that you happened to be at the scene that day, because someone deliberately made it so you'd be there...\"\n[2397] She interrupted me. [2398] \"He thinks I staged it all, that it's all a made-up story. He thinks Ina must have known something, like Ina recognizing that it was me imitating Miss Pemaroo's voice on the phone.\" [2399] \"Was it your voice?\" [2400] \"No, of course not. I never made that call. I told you that already.\" [2401] \"Look at me, Hillary,\" I said. \"Whatever you say to others, you have to tell me the truth.\" [2402] \"So, you don't believe me either!\" [2403] \"No, I do. You might have made that call for a very innocent reason. Someone told you to do it, maybe told you it was just a joke, and then you got scared, but once you lie, you have to keep lying. Is that how it was?\" [2404] \"No, no, no! How many times do I have to tell you?\" [2405] \"Well, Hillary, you do seem to be hiding something. I hope you can trust me. If Hakaste has anything against you, he hasn't told me anything he knows\u2014\"\n[2406] She interrupted me again. [2407] \"You want him to tell you everything?\" [2408] \"Well, he has no reason not to. We're partners on the same side.\" [2409] Just then, the waiter came over with our order. The coffee tasted as bland as the recently popular beaver fur coats. [2410] \"I didn't know you were involved with the police,\" Hillary said, stirring her coffee slowly. [2411] \"I'm not really a cop. I come from a completely different industry. But I can understand if Dick didn't tell me about you for a particular reason. Because he likes you. Well, I like you. More than like you, maybe. I'm doing this for you, Hill, no matter what you've done. You were terrified when you ran out of the house that day. You were genuinely scared. You weren't faking it. You couldn't pull that off.\" [2412] \"Of course I was scared. I was terrified.\" [2413] \"Was it just seeing the body that scared you, or was there something else?\" [2414] \"What else could there be?\" [2415] I perked up. [2416] \"Why did you steal the clock with the inscription 'Rosemary'?\" [2417] \"What are you talking about? Why would I steal it?\" [2418] \"I'm asking you why you did it.\" [2419] \"I never touched it.\" [2420] \"You said you were going back inside because you forgot your gloves. You didn't wear gloves that day. It was a warm September day. I've never seen you wear gloves. Then, you went back inside and took the clock. Don't lie to me. You did take it, didn't you?\" [2421] She paused for a moment, breaking the scones in the dish. [2422] \"Okay,\" she said softly, almost whispering. \"Okay. I did it. I took the clock and just shoved it into my bag, and then I left.\" [2423] \"But why did you do that?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[453] \"Alright, alright,\" Dick said. \"You win.\" [454] Chapter 5\n[455] \"Mom!\" O'Neil Courtin stopped and said, sliding a small metal rocket model up and down the windowpane to simulate the rocket's journey through space and into the orbit of Venus, \"Mom, what are you thinking?\" [456] Mrs. Cortin, a stern-faced woman, was busy washing ceramic dishes in the sink and didn't respond. [457] \"Mom, a police car is parked outside our house.\" [458] \"Didn't I tell you not to lie anymore, O'Neil?\" Mrs. Cortin said, roughly placing the dishes on the drying rack. \"Do you remember what I told you before?\" [459] \"I'm not lying,\" O'Neil insisted, \"there really is a police car, and two men got out of it.\" [460] Mrs. Cortin suddenly turned to her child. [461] \"What have you done?\" she demanded. \"Bringing shame and disgrace upon us? That's what you've done!\" [462] \"Of course not,\" Oney said. \"I didn't do anything.\" [463] \"It's those Alf and his gang,\" Mrs. Korting said. \"He and his cronies. A bunch of thugs! I told you, your father told you, they're no good. They always get into trouble. First, they'll be brought before the juvenile court, then sent to a juvenile detention center. I don't want you to turn out like that, do you hear?\" [464] \"They're at the front door,\" Oney said loudly. [465] Mrs. Korting put down her work in the sink and walked over to the window next to her child. [466] \"Hmm,\" she grumbled. [467] Just then, there was a knock at the door. Mrs. Cording quickly wiped her hands with a tea towel and went through the hallway to open the door. She looked at the two men on the doorstep with contempt and suspicion. [468] \"Are you Mrs. Cording?\" the taller man asked politely. [469] \"Yes,\" said Mrs. Cording. [470] \"Can I come in? I'm Detective Hakaste.\" [471] Mrs. Corton reluctantly moved back. She opened the door and gestured for the detective to come in. The room was spotlessly clean, giving the impression that few visitors were welcomed, and this was indeed the case. [472] Enoch, curious, came out of the kitchen and quietly leaned against the door. [473] \"Your son?\" Detective Inspector Hakast asked. [474] \"Yes,\" Mrs. Corton said, defending him with a defiant tone, \"He's a good boy, not what you think.\" [475] \"I believe him to be,\" Detective Inspector Hakast said politely. [476] Mrs. Cottington's hostility subsided. [477] \"I came to ask you a few questions about 19 Wilbraham Crescent. I understand you work there.\" [478] \"I haven't denied that,\" Mrs. Cottington said, still not completely recovered from her previous emotions. [479] \"You work for Miss Millechamp.\" [480] \"Yes, Miss Millechamp is a very kind lady.\" [481] \"Blind,\" said Inspector Hakast. [482] \"Yes, poor thing. But perhaps you didn't know, she has a pair of magic hands, and she can easily cope with life through touch. She can even go out on the street and cross the road by herself. She's different from some people I know who are always whining about nothing.\" [483] \"Do you usually work there in the morning?\" [484] \"Yes. I get there around nine-thirty to ten, and leave at twelve, or whenever I finish my work.\" [485] \"On the contrary,\" thought the inspector, thinking of the four clocks. [486] Mrs. Corten looked at him puzzled. [487] \"What happened?\" she asked. [488] \"A man was found dead in the living room of 19 Wilbraham Crescent this afternoon,\" the detective said. [489] Mrs. Corten's eyes widened. Eunice Corten twisted and turned in excitement, saying \"Oh\" with her mouth open, but then closed it again when she realized someone might see her. [490] \"Dead?\" Mrs. Corten couldn't believe it. And even more puzzling was, \"In the living room?\" [491] \"Yes, he was stabbed,\" he said. [492] \"You mean someone killed him?\" she asked. [493] \"Yes, he was killed,\" he replied. [494] \"Who did it?\" Mrs. Courting pressed. [495] \"That's all we know so far,\" said Huckleberry. \"We thought maybe you could help us.\" [496] \"I had nothing to do with the murder,\" Mrs. Kittredge said firmly. [497] \"Not about the murder, but there are one or two things we'd like to ask you. Did anyone come to see you this morning?\" [498] \"I don't remember anyone. No, not today. Who was he?\" [499] \"He was about sixty, wearing a well-dressed dark suit. He may have claimed to be an insurance agent for a company.\" [500] \"I wouldn't let him in,\" Mrs. Kittredge said. \"There's no insurance agent, and no one selling vacuum cleaners or encyclopedias. Pembroke wouldn't tolerate them, and neither would I.\" [501] \"According to the information on the card he brought, his name is Mr. Corley. Have you ever heard of that name?\" [502] \"Corley? Corley?\" Mrs. Cortlandt shook her head. \"Sounds like an Indian name.\u201d She guessed. [503] \"Oh, no,\" Hercule said, \"he's not Indian.\" [504] \"Who found him, Miss Pemerton?\" [505] \"A young lady, a stenographer, who came here by mistake, thinking she was sent to work for Miss Pemerton. She found the body. Miss Pemerton came home almost at the same time.\" [506] Mrs. Huttington sighed deeply. [507] \"How could this happen,\" she said, \"how could this happen!\" [508] \"We would like to ask you to look at the body of this man, at your convenience, and tell us if you have seen him on Wilbraham Crescent or if he ever visited Miss Pemarhu. Miss Pemarhu is quite certain he never came to her house. There are a few points I would like to ask you about, can you remember how many clocks there were in her sitting room?\" [509] Mrs. Huttington hesitated not at all. [510] \"The big clock was in the corner, they called it a grandfather clock; and there was a cuckoo clock on the wall, with a bird that would pop out and say 'cuckoo.' It didn't startle you, did it?\" She added quickly, \"I never touched any of them, never. Miss Pemarhu liked to wind them herself.\" [511] \"Those two clocks are all right,\" said the detective, reassuring her. \"Are you sure there were only those two in the room this morning?\" [512] \"Of course. Should there have been more?\" [513] \"For example, was there a square silver clock, a traveling clock? Or a gilded little clock on a shelf, or a ceramic clock with a patterned face, or a leather clock with the word 'Rosemary' written on one side?\" [514] \"Certainly not. I have never seen any of those things.\" [515] \"If they were there, you would have noticed them, wouldn't you?\" [516] \"Certainly.\" [517] \"These four clocks are all faster than the cuckoo clock and the grandfather clock by an hour and a half.\" [518] \"Maybe they're foreign clocks,\" Mrs. Cochran said. \"My husband and I went on a trip to Switzerland and Italy, and their time was an hour ahead of ours. It must have something to do with the Common Market. I'm not in favor of the Common Market, and neither is Mr. Cochran. I think British things are good enough for me.\" [519] Detective Hercule Poirot didn't want to discuss politics. [520] \"Can you tell me the exact time you left Miss Pemuk's house this morning?\" [521] \"Twelve fifteen, that's the time,\" Mrs. Kettle said. [522] \"Was Miss Pemuk at home then?\" [523] \"No, she wasn't. She hasn't come back yet. She usually comes back between twelve and twelve thirty, but sometimes she doesn't.\" [524] \"When did she leave home?\" [525] \"Before I got there. I got there at ten o'clock.\" [526] \"Thank you, Mrs. Cottington.\" [527] \"These clocks do look rather strange,\" said Mrs. Cottington. \"Perhaps Miss Pembroke went to an auction market. They're antiques, aren't they? Based on your description, they seem like it.\" [528] \"Does Miss Pembroke often go to auction markets?\" [529] \"Four months ago, she bought a roll of mountain wool carpet at an auction market. The quality was very good. She told me it was very cheap. She also bought some silk curtains. Although they needed to be trimmed a bit, they looked brand new.\" [530] \"But she doesn't usually buy small antiques, paintings, or ceramics like that at auction markets, does she?\" [531] Mrs. Kudin shook her head. [532] \"I haven't seen her do that since I've known her, but of course, it's hard to say about auction markets, isn't it? I mean, you lose your self-control there. When you get home, you'll be thinking, 'What am I doing buying these things?' I've bought six jam jars. I keep thinking I could have gotten them cheaper. The cups and plates are the same. I could have gotten better ones at the Tuesday market.\" [533] She shook her head slightly. Detective Hakaste felt he had no more questions to ask and left. Then Oona began to reflect on the topic they had just discussed. [534] \"Murder! Oh my!\" said Oona. [535] The idea of conquering space was immediately overshadowed by the exciting conversation of the day. [536] \"Miss Pemaroo couldn't have done that, could she?\" he ventured to analyze. [537] \"Don't talk nonsense,\" said his mother. An idea flashed through her mind. \"I don't know whether I should tell him\u2014\"\n[538] \"Tell him what, mom?\" [539] \"It's none of your business,\" said Mrs. Kettlety. \"In fact, there's nothing to tell.\" [540] Chapter Six\n[541] Colin Lumb's Narrative\n[542] 1\n[543] After having two half-cooked steaks and a pint of beer outside, Dick Hakaste satisfiedly sighed and announced that he felt better, then said,\n[544] \"Dead insurance agents, strange clocks, and screaming girls, go to hell! Let's talk about you, Colin. I thought you disappeared from the world. I didn't expect to see you wandering around Croton's back street. I can assure you that marine biologists won't find their calling in Croton.\" [545] \"Do you look down on marine biology, Dick? It's a useful profession. But it's so boring to talk about that people get annoyed when you mention it, so you don't need to explain further.\" [546] \"No chance to show yourself off, eh?\" [547] \"You forget,\" I said coldly, \"I'm an oceanographer. I have a degree in the subject from Cambridge University. Not very good, but at least it's a degree. It's an interesting profession, and someday I'll go back to it.\" [548] \"Of course, I know what you've been doing,\" Hackerst said. \"Congratulations. The trial of Rackin is next month, isn't it?\" [549] \"Yes.\" [550] \"It's amazing how he managed to smuggle the information out. I thought someone would suspect him.\" [551] \"No one would suspect him,\" I said. [552] \"He must be smart,\" Dick commented. [553] I shook my head. [554] \"No, I don't think he's smart. In fact, I think he just did what he was told to do. He could get his hands on very important documents. He took them out with him, had them photographed, gave them back, and then they were sent back to their original location the same day. Everything was arranged in a very tight and compact manner. He developed a habit of having lunch in different places. We thought he always hung his coat in the same place, but it was actually a coat identical to his, and the man wearing it was never the same person. The coat was switched, but the man who switched it never spoke to Rake, and Rake never spoke to him. We wanted to know more technical details. This plan was flawless, and the timing was precise. This person is indeed very smart.\" [555] \"That's why you're still hanging around the Portsmouth Naval Base, right?\" [556] \"Yes, we know the location in the naval base and the location in London. We know when and where Rackin collects his salary and how he collects it. But there's a gap between these two points. There's a subtle and delicate organization between them. That's where we want to know more, because that's where the brainpower comes into play. There's a great command center here, a route that will make you doubtful not once, but seven or eight times. It's a superb plan.\" [557] \"Why did Rackin do all this?\" Hakast asked curiously, \"For political ideals? Promoting individualism? Or for quick money?\" [558] \"He's not an idealist,\" I said. \"I think it's 'just for money'.\" [559] \"If you had started from that angle earlier, wouldn't you have found him faster? He must have spent the money. He doesn't save money.\" [560] \"Oh, that's right! He spent all the money. In fact, I was a bit hasty in dealing with him.\" [561] Hakast nodded understandingly. [562] \"I see. You fell once and then gradually used him. Right?\" [563] \"More or less. Before we found him, he had already taken out some valuable intelligence, so we let him leak more intelligence, obviously very valuable. In my workplace, we sometimes have to act like idiots.\" [564] \"I don't like your job, Colin,\" Hakast said thoughtfully. [565] \"It's not the kind of exciting job people think it is,\" I said. \"In fact, it's usually monotonous, but not just boring. Now people have a feeling that there is nothing that is truly secret. We know their secrets, and they know ours. Our agents are often their agents, and their agents are often ours. In the end, whoever is the double agent, it becomes a nightmare! Sometimes, I think, everyone knows each other's secrets, but they set up conspiracies and pretend not to know.\" [566] \"I see what you mean,\" Dick said kindly. [567] He looked at me curiously. [568] \"I know why you're still in Potterbury. But Croton is ten miles away from Potterbury.\" [569] \"Actually, my real purpose is,\" I said, \"New Moon Street.\" [570] \"New Moon Street?\" Hakast looked puzzled. [571] \"Yes. Or, the moon, the new moon, the rising moon, etc. I started my exploration from Portbury. There's a pub called 'New Moon' there. I spent a lot of time there. It sounds a bit crazy. Then there's 'Moon and Stars', 'Rising Moon', 'Happy Scythe', and 'Cross and Crescent' \u2013 that one's in a place called Simde. Nothing came of it. So I gave up on the moon pubs and started studying crescent-shaped streets. There are a few of them in Portbury. There's Lancelbury Crescent, Addington Crescent, Lifemide Crescent, and Victoria Crescent.\" [572] Suddenly, I saw a confused expression on Dick's face and started laughing. [573] \"Don't look so bewildered, Dick. There's something real waiting for me to start.\" [574] I took out my wallet and pulled out a piece of paper from it, handing it to him. It was a hotel stationery paper, on which a sketch was drawn. [575] \"This is something a boy named Hambley had in his wallet. He put a lot of effort into the case of Racking. He's a good guy, really good. He was hit by a car in London and died. The driver fled. No one saw the license plate. I don't know what this is, but it's something Hambley hastily wrote down or copied, thinking it was important. It made him think of something? Did he see or hear anything? Maybe something related to the moon or the new moon, and the number 61 and the capital letter M. After his death, this note fell into my hands. I don't know what I'm looking for, but I'm sure I'll find something. I don't know what 61 represents, or what M means. I've been busy for three weeks now, within a radius centered on Portbury. I haven't found anything. Cromwell is within my plans. That's it. To be honest, Dick, I don't have much hope for Cromwell. There's only one New Moon Street there, which is Wilbraham New Moon Street. Before asking you if you found any information that can help me, I'm going to walk along Wilbraham New Moon Street first and see what the number 61 looks like. That's what I did this afternoon, but I couldn't find number 61.\" [576] \"I told you, 61 is occupied by a local architect.\" [577] \"That's not what I'm looking for. Are there any foreigners living there?\" [578] \"There might be. Many people do these days. If there are, they'll be listed. I'll check for you tomorrow.\" [579] \"Thank you, Dick.\" [580] \"I'll be doing routine inquiries to the neighbors on either side of 19 tomorrow. Asking if they've seen anyone go to that house, and so on. I'll go to the house behind 19, the one whose garden connects with 19's. I seem to remember that 61 is right behind 19. If you'd like, I can take you along.\" [581] I eagerly agreed. [582] \"I'll be your RAMS officer, in charge of taking notes.\" [583] We agreed to meet at the police station at 9:30 the next morning. [584] 2\n[585] On the second day, I arrived at the appointed time and found my friend seething with anger. [586] After he sent away his sulking subordinate, I cautiously asked him what had happened. [587] For a moment, Hakaste seemed unable to speak. After a while, he blurted out, \"It's those damn clocks again!\" [588] \"Clocks again? What's the matter this time?\" [589] \"One of them is missing.\" [590] \"Missing? Which one?\" [591] \"The leather travel clock. The one with 'Rosemary' written on it.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThere is an important statement.\" [3092] \"What?\" I asked. \"Who said it? When?\" [3093] \"At the appropriate time, dear.\" [3094] \"Please tell me, Monsieur Poirot,\" the inspector politely returned to the topic. [3095] \"If you draw a circle around number 19, all the people inside it could have killed Mr. Corry. Mrs. Hemm, the Brandt couple, the MacNodon couple, Miss Watrous. But most importantly, some people have already been set up in the scene. Miss Pemerton may have killed him before going out at about 1:35 p.m., and Miss Webb may have been arranged to meet him, killing him before running out of the room to call the police.\" [3096] \"Ah,\" said the detective, \"now you're getting to the specifics.\" [3097] \"Of course,\" said Poirot, sliding his wheelchair, \"you, my dear Colwyn. You were also on the scene. Finding the lower half of the house on the upper half of the street.\" [3098] \"Oh, really?\" I said indignantly, \"What do you mean?\" [3099] \"I mean that I know all there is to know!\" Poirot declared pompously. [3100] \"And I'm the one who's still trying to tell you the whole thing!\" [3101] \"Murderers are usually arrogant,\" Poirot pointed out, \"and may well play games with you so you have a reason to laugh at me.\" [3102] \"If you go on like this, you'll convince me,\" I said. [3103] I began to feel a little uneasy. [3104] Poirot turned to Inspector Hercule. [3105] \"I say to myself, essentially, it's a simple case of murder. The appearance of the clock, the time advanced by an hour, the deliberate discovery of the body, all of this must be put aside. The essential thing is that an ordinary middle-aged man has died and that someone wanted him to die. If we knew who the dead man was, it would suggest who the murderer is. If he was a notorious con artist, we would have to find those he may have cheated; if he was a detective, we would look for those who had committed a crime under his nose; if he was a rich man, we would look among his heirs. But if we don't know who the man was, finding a suspect from this vast pool is a very daunting task.\" [3106] \"Without considering Miss Pemukh and Miss Hila Webber, who should not have been there, the answer is disappointing. I think only Mr. Lymes has something strange?\" Poirot looked at me inquiringly, and I nodded. \"Everyone has a reason to be trusted. Brand is a famous local architect, MacNorton is a professor at Cambridge University, Mrs. Hamm is the widow of a local auctioneer, and the Waterhouse siblings are respected locals. So let's go back to Mr. Curry. Where does he come from? What brought him to 19 Wilbraham Crescent? The neighbor, Mrs. Hamm, said something very revealing when she found out that the dead man was not living at number 19. 'Oh! I see. He came here to die. How strange.' She has a talent for seeing the essence of things, a talent that only those who are only concerned with their own actions and do not care about others possess. She summed up the whole case. Mr. Curry came to 19 Wilbraham Crescent to die. That's all there is to it!\" [3107] \"Her words suddenly startled me,\" I said. [3108] Poirot did not notice me. [3109] \" 'Dear, dear, dear, come to die.' Mr. Curry came and was killed. But that's not all. His identity cannot be confirmed, which is important. He had no wallet, no identification, and the brand name on his clothes had been torn off. But that's not enough. The card of an insurance agent named Curry was just a temporary measure. If the man's identity cannot be confirmed, he will eventually be given a false identity. It could be his brother, his sister, or his wife. As for the wife, Mrs. Rivale, just the name may have already aroused suspicion. There is a village called Curry-Rivale in Somerset, where I and a friend live nearby \u2013 I don't know what the two names suggest, Mr. Curry, Mrs. Rivale.\" [3110] \"At this point, the plan is almost clear, but what confuses me is why our killer assumes that the police will never be able to determine the actual identity of the deceased. If the man had no family, at least there would be a butler, a servant, or a business partner. This leads me to another assumption: no one knows that the man has disappeared. Another assumption is that he is not British, but only a traveler in this country. This fits with the fact that he had traces of having his teeth treated, but there was no record of any treatment.\" [3111] \"My mind began to form a vague picture of the victim and the killer. The case was carefully planned and skillfully executed, but now there is a flaw that the killer did not anticipate.\" [3112] \"What?\" Hercule asked. [3113] To my surprise, at this point Poirot tilted his head back dramatically and recited a poem:\n[3114] A nail lost, a shoe lost,\n[3115] A shoe lost, a horse lost,\n[3116] One war horse was lost, one war was lost,\n[3117] One war was lost, one empire was lost,\n[3118] All because one horseshoe nail was missing. [3119] He leaned forward. [3120] \"There may be many suspects for the murder of Mr. Curry. But there is only one who could have killed, or had a reason to kill, the girl Iona.\" [3121] We all turned to him at the same time. [3122] \"Let's consider Cavenish Typewriting Agency. There are eight girls working there. On September 9th, four of them were out on an errand, so they wouldn't be back until after lunch. Normally, they would have lunch between 12:30 and 1:30 p.m. The other four, Hilary Webb, Ingrid Brent, and two others, would have lunch between 1:30 and 2:30 p.m. But on that day, Ingrid Brent had a small accident on her way back to the office. Her shoe heel got stuck in the grating, making it difficult for her to walk. So she bought some bread and went straight back to the office.\" [3123] Poirot wagged his strong finger at us. [3124] \"We know that Ingrid Brent was worried about something. She tried to meet Hilary Webb outside the office, but failed. Could this be related to Hilary Webb? We couldn't find any evidence. She might have just wanted to ask Hilary Webb about something that was bothering her. But there was one thing that was clear. She wanted to talk to Hilary Webb outside the office.\" [3125] \"Her statement to the police, that morning, was the only clue we had to what was bothering her. She said, 'I don't know if what she said is true.' Three women testified that day. Ingrid might have been referring to Miss Pemshaw. Or, as we had previously surmised, she might have been referring to Hilary Webb. But there was a third possibility. She might have been referring to Miss Martin.\" [3126] \"Miss Martin? But her testimony only lasted a few minutes.\" [3127] \"Indeed, she only stated the phone call she received claiming it was from Miss Pemerton.\" [3128] \"Are you saying that Ina knew that the phone call was not from Miss Pemerton?\" [3129] \"My inference is even simpler. I believe there was no such phone call at all.\" [3130] He continued speaking:\n[3131] \"Ina's heel broke off. The grille was close to the office. So she returned to the typing pool quickly. But Miss Martindeer was in her own office, unaware that Ina had returned. As far as she was concerned, there was only herself in the office at that time. All she had to do was say that a call came in at 1:49. Ina did not know the severity of the situation at first. Hill went to see Miss Martindeer, who was told there was an appointment to go out. The nature and time of the appointment, Ina did not know. [3132] \"Then the news of the murder broke, and the story unfolded little by little. Miss Pemarho called and asked Hill to come to her. But Miss Pemarho said she had not made the call. The call was said to have come at 2:50 p.m. But Ina knew it was not true. There was no call at that time. Miss Martindeer must have been mistaken, but Miss Martindeer was sure she was not mistaken. The more Ina thought about it, the more puzzled she became. She had to ask Hill. Hill might know. [3133] \"Then the interrogation began. All the girls attended. Miss Martindeer repeated her statement about the call, but Ina knew that the clear evidence and precise time she provided were false. So she went to ask the officer to speak to the chief inspector. I think it's likely that Miss Martindeer overheard her question as she left with the crowd. Or perhaps she heard the girls talking about Ina breaking her heel at that moment, realizing the truth. Anyway, she followed the girl to Wilbraham Crescent. I wonder why Ina went there? [3134] \"Just to see the scene of the murder, I guess,\" Hakaste sighed. [3135] \"Yes, that's right. Maybe Miss Martindeer was talking to her, and they were walking down the street together when Ina casually expressed her doubts. Miss Martindeer decided to take immediate action. They happened to be walking past a phone booth. She said, 'This is very important. You must call the police immediately. Tell them we are going to see them right now.' Following someone's instructions was a habit Ina had formed. She went inside, picked up the phone, and Miss Martindeer followed her, standing behind her, tightening her scarf around her neck, and choking her to death.\" [3136] \"Did nobody see?\" [3137] Poirot shrugged. [3138] \"It should have been someone who saw! But it was exactly 1:00. Lunchtime. And people on New Moon Street were busy looking at this and that. This gave the bold and shameless person the opportunity to act.\" [3139] Hercule Poirot shook his head, incredulous. [3140] \"Miss Martin? I don't understand how she got involved in this case?\" [3141] \"Indeed, it was not immediately apparent. But there is no doubt that Miss Marple killed Ingrid. Oh, yes. Only she could have killed Ingrid, so she must be involved. From Miss Marple, I began to suspect that this murder was a Macbethian[1] one, a cruel and unimaginative boring woman.\u201d\n[3142] \"Lacking imagination?\" Hercule Poirot asked. [3143] \"Oh, yes, very lacking. But very efficient. A first-rate conspirator.\u201d\n[3144] \"But the motive? What was her reason for doing it?\" [3145] Hercule Poirot looks at me. He wags a finger. [3146] \"So, your neighbors' gossip was of no use to you, was it? I found a statement that intrigued me. Do you remember when talking about living abroad, Mrs. Brand said she liked living in Crofton because she had an aunt there. But Mrs. Brand actually had no aunt. She inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada, who was her only surviving relative in the family.\" [3147] Hercule Poirot sat up alertly. [3148] \"So you think \u2013\"\n[3149] Poirot leaned back in his chair, folding his fingers together. He half-closed his eyes, as if dreaming. [3150] \"For example, you are a man, a very ordinary, cautious man, with financial difficulties. One day, you receive a letter from a law firm saying that your wife has inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada. The letter was addressed to Mrs. Brand, but the problem is that the Mrs. Brand who received the letter was not the one mentioned in the letter. She was the second wife, not the first. Imagine how sad and frustrating that would be! But suddenly, there's a solution. Who would know that this was not Mrs. Brand? In Crofton, no one knew that Mr. Brand had been married before. His first marriage was many years ago, during the war, when he was overseas. His wife died soon after they married, and he remarried quickly. He had the original marriage certificate, various other documents, and photos of all the relatives in Canada. Everything was in order. Anyway, it was worth taking a risk. They tried, and succeeded. They went through all the legal procedures. The Brand couple became rich, and their financial difficulties ended \u2013\"\n[3151] \"However, a year later, something unexpected happened to them. What happened? I assume that someone from Canada came to this country and knew all the details about the first Mrs. Brand, and the truth was about to come out. He could be an elderly family executor or a close friend of the family, but whoever he is, he would know. Perhaps they thought they could avoid meeting. Mrs. Brand could pretend to be sick, or she could go abroad, but any similar thing would arouse suspicion. The visitor would insist on seeing the woman he had come to visit specially.\" [3152] \"So, they decided to murder him?\" [3153] \"Yes. Here, I think Mrs. Brand's sister is the mastermind behind the plot.\" [3154] \"You mean Miss Martin and Mrs. Brand are sisters?\" [3155] \"Only then does the story make sense.\" [3156] \"When I saw Mrs. Brand, she reminded me of someone,\" said Hercule Poirot, \"although their personalities were very different, but upon careful reflection, they were somewhat alike. But how could they hope to escape? Someone is missing. They would face interrogation \u2013\"\n[3157] \"If this person went abroad, perhaps just for a vacation, not for business, his schedule would not be fixed. Receiving letters from one place, then postcards from another. By the time people start to suspect, a long time may have passed. Who would associate the already-identified Harry Castleton with the wealthy tourist from Canada who never even set foot in the country? If I were the killer, I would go to France or Belgium for a carefree day of sightseeing, and then deliberately throw the dead man's passport in a train or a car, so that the interrogation could take place there.\" [3158] I unconsciously moved, and Poirot stared at me intently. [3159] \"Right?\" he said. [3160] \"Brand told me he had gone to Bournemouth for a day trip with a blonde woman, and I thought \u2013\"\n[3161] \"That's perfectly normal. Don't doubt it, it's his old habit.\" [3162] \"But these are just conjectures,\" Hercule countered. [3163] \"But you can do your own investigation,\" Hercule said. [3164] He picked up a hotel stationery sheet from the shelf in front of him and handed it to Hercule. [3165] \"You can write to Mr. Endebie at 10 Innisfree Gardens, SW7, who has promised to investigate for me in Canada. He is a well-known international lawyer.\" [3166] \"What about those clocks then?\" [3167] \"Oh! Those clocks! Those remarkable clocks!\" Poirot laughed. \"I think you will find that Miss Rosemary will be held responsible for them. Hence this case, as I said, is quite simple, only made to look fantastic. That clock that Miss Hyde took to be repaired. Did she leave it at the typewriter repair shop? Miss Marple took advantage of this to base her crazy talk on it, perhaps because of that clock, she chose Miss Hyde to be the one who discovered the body\u2014?\" [3168] Hercule suddenly exclaimed loudly:\n[3169] \"You still say this woman is boring and lacks imagination? When did she start planning all this?\" [3170] \"But these were not her plans. That's what makes it interesting. Everything was here, waiting for her. From the very beginning, I discovered this modus operandi, which I was familiar with because I had been reading about it. Very lucky. As Colin will tell you, this week I attended an auction of author's manuscripts. There were some works by Gary Greggson, and I didn't hold out much hope. But luck was on my side. Here\u2014\" Suddenly, he pulled out two old notebooks from the drawer of the table. \"\u2014it's all here! All the plots he planned to write in these books. He died before he could finish them. But Miss Marple, as his secretary, knew all the plots. She could use them to serve her purposes if she wanted to.\" [3171] \"But these clocks must have meant something \u2013 in Mr. Greggson's story, I think.\" [3172] \"Yes, that's right. His clocks were set at five minutes and four seconds, five minutes and seven seconds, and five minutes and five seconds. Together, they form a safe's password, 514547. The safe was hidden behind a replica of the Mona Lisa painting. Inside the safe, \"Poirot continued grumpily, \"was the Russian royal crown. And that's how everything was explained! It was Miss Marple's plot. An innocent girl being framed. Oh, yes, it was easy for Miss Marple. She just had to choose some local characters and make them act according to the plot. So these clear clues led you nowhere! Yes, she is a clever woman. I wonder if Mr. Greggson left her any inheritance? How did he die and what caused his death? I'm curious.\" [3173] Hercule didn't want to hear any more about the past. He put away the notebook and took the hotel business card from my hand. Staring at it, I realized how foolish I had been. [3174] \"Thank you, Poirot, sir,\" Hercule said. \"Your words have given us a lot of inspiration.\" [3175] \"The End\"\n[3176] \"If I have been of any help, I would be very happy.\" [3177] Polo appeared humble. [3178] \"I need to check all the facts -\"\n[3179] \"Of course, of course -\"\n[3180] After bidding farewell, Hercast left. [3181] Poirot turned to me. He frowned. [3182] \"Cheer up. I want to ask you, what's wrong? You look so gloomy.\" [3183] \"I realized how stupid I've been.\" [3184] \"Ah, that's alright. Many people are like that.\" [3185] \"But not you, Hercule Poirot!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."